('46a00ecd-c60a-53c6-92e9-dba4b1cd0705', '481dfd93-9742-5bad-80df-82cc132d35c2', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Branching on a condition$c$, $c$An `if` statement evaluates its condition, converts the result to a boolean, and runs the block only when that boolean is `true`. Add `else` for the other path and `else if` to test further conditions in order; the first branch whose condition is truthy wins and the rest are skipped, so put the most specific test first.

The conditional operator `condition ? a : b` is an expression: it produces a value, which makes it ideal for assigning one of two values or returning one of two results. Nesting it, or using it to run side effects, makes code hard to scan, so treat it as a tool for short choices only.

`switch` compares one value against a list of `case` labels using strict equality (`===`). Execution starts at the first matching case and continues into the following cases until it meets `break` or the end of the block. That fall-through is deliberate and occasionally useful, for example when several cases share one body, but forgetting `break` is one of the most common beginner bugs. A `default` label catches values that match nothing.

Whichever form you use, aim for branches that a reader can check off: every possible input should land somewhere obvious.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8047e5b7-2867-5403-a57f-5a745c1c7038', '481dfd93-9742-5bad-80df-82cc132d35c2', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Ordered conditions$c$, $c$The order of the chain matters. Testing `>= 90` before `>= 75` keeps each branch simple.$c$, $c$function grade(score) {
  if (score >= 90) return 'A';
  else if (score >= 75) return 'B';
  else if (score >= 60) return 'C';
  return 'Needs work';
}

console.log(grade(91)); // 'A'
console.log(grade(75)); // 'B'
console.log(grade(12)); // 'Needs work'$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('4a22c7a5-542c-540b-b612-0605c01a73b5', '481dfd93-9742-5bad-80df-82cc132d35c2', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$switch with grouped cases$c$, $c$Two cases share a body by omitting `break` between them. `default` handles anything else.$c$, $c$function shippingDays(region) {
  switch (region) {
    case 'metro':
    case 'city':
      return 1;
    case 'rural':
      return 4;
    default:
      return 7;
  }
}

console.log(shippingDays('city'));  // 1
console.log(shippingDays('island')); // 7$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8acf78a9-0dbc-5c01-9244-1091a4ac8c47', '481dfd93-9742-5bad-80df-82cc132d35c2', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Writing `if (x = 5)` (assignment) when `if (x === 5)` (comparison) was intended. The assignment succeeds and the condition is always truthy.
- Forgetting `break` in `switch`, so a match runs every later case as well.
- Comparing with `==` in an `if` and being surprised that `'0'` equals `0`.
- Deeply nested conditionals where a lookup object or an early return would be clearer.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('e62e2ea4-2a54-5803-a700-caa193888517', '481dfd93-9742-5bad-80df-82cc132d35c2', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Interviewers rarely ask about `if` directly, but they watch whether you cover every branch, whether you reach for `switch` when a plain object lookup would be clearer, and whether you can explain `switch` fall-through without hesitation. A crisp answer: "switch uses strict equality and falls through until it hits break."$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7ef82f17-7224-5eba-b0e6-77f2fd274258', '481dfd93-9742-5bad-80df-82cc132d35c2', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$Apply ordered conditions in the **FizzBuzz variant** problem, where the order of your checks decides whether the answer is right.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"fizzbuzz-variant"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('c03b6c03-f9fa-5540-b2e8-bc43184ab3c6', 'fdabe8a8-d7a2-51f1-ab46-462dc274993b', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Four loops, four intents$c$, $c$A classic `for` loop has three parts: an initialiser that runs once, a condition checked before every iteration, and an update that runs after each iteration. It is the right choice when you need the index itself or a custom step.

`while` repeats as long as its condition is truthy and is natural when you do not know the number of iterations in advance, for example reading until a queue is empty. `do...while` runs the body at least once before checking.

`for...of` iterates the *values* of anything iterable: arrays, strings, Maps, Sets, and more. It is the cleanest way to say "for each item". `for...in` iterates the *enumerable string keys* of an object, including inherited ones, and its order is not guaranteed for arrays, so avoid it for arrays.

`break` leaves the loop immediately; `continue` skips to the next iteration. Both are fine when they make the loop simpler, but if you find yourself using several, consider extracting a function and returning early instead.

Because `let` creates a fresh binding for each iteration, callbacks created inside a `for (let i …)` loop each remember their own `i`. With `var`, they would all share one.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7a82f3a5-3188-5659-a522-eec9a016b007', 'fdabe8a8-d7a2-51f1-ab46-462dc274993b', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Index versus value$c$, $c$Use the index when you need positions; use `for...of` when you only need values.$c$, $c$const prices = [120, 80, 45];

for (let i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
  console.log(`#${i + 1}: ${prices[i]}`);
}

let total = 0;
for (const price of prices) total += price;
console.log(total); // 245$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('400f1166-10a3-592c-95fb-210e0b9094d3', 'fdabe8a8-d7a2-51f1-ab46-462dc274993b', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Stopping early$c$, $c$`break` ends the search as soon as a match is found, avoiding needless work.$c$, $c$const orders = ['paid', 'paid', 'failed', 'paid'];
let firstFailure = -1;

for (let i = 0; i < orders.length; i++) {
  if (orders[i] === 'failed') {
    firstFailure = i;
    break;
  }
}
console.log(firstFailure); // 2$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('a6a25742-e664-5ecc-a6fe-55eaa6067a46', 'fdabe8a8-d7a2-51f1-ab46-462dc274993b', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Off-by-one errors: `i <= arr.length` reads one past the end and yields `undefined`.
- Using `for...in` on arrays and getting string keys (`'0'`, `'1'`) plus any added properties.
- Mutating the array you are iterating (removing items shifts later indexes).
- Infinite `while` loops because the condition never changes inside the body.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('6a8273f2-ce56-57fa-a2b0-a787705bca0c', 'fdabe8a8-d7a2-51f1-ab46-462dc274993b', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Expect questions such as "what is the difference between for...of and for...in?" and output-prediction snippets that close over a loop variable declared with `let` versus `var`. Being able to say that `let` gives each iteration its own binding is a strong signal.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('dc7ae8a5-b8fc-51d3-b268-f4194124ee77', 'fdabe8a8-d7a2-51f1-ab46-462dc274993b', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$Try **Sum of multiples**, which rewards choosing the right loop and stopping condition.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"sum-of-multiples"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('f24b45b4-3cab-5b15-9995-43db8ab8d125', '19f9be40-6bce-5d35-a660-2013f818759c', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Handle the edges first$c$, $c$A guard clause is an `if` at the top of a function that deals with an unusual or invalid input and returns (or throws) immediately. Everything after it can assume the input is normal, so the main path stays at one level of indentation. Code reviewers like this because the reader sees the assumptions up front instead of discovering them in a final `else`.

Early return works with any exit: `return`, `throw`, `continue` in a loop, or `break`. The habit is to ask "what must be true for the rest of this function to make sense?" and check those things at the top.

A related refactor is replacing a chain of `if (x === 'a') … else if (x === 'b') …` with a plain object or `Map` that maps inputs to outputs. When the branches only differ by data, a lookup is shorter, easier to extend, and easier to test.

The goal is not to minimise lines. It is to make the happy path obvious and the edge cases explicit.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('adc1b0b3-32cd-558d-806e-5f81f218aead', '19f9be40-6bce-5d35-a660-2013f818759c', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Before and after$c$, $c$The nested version hides the main work three levels deep. The guarded version reads top to bottom.$c$, $c$// Nested
function discountNested(user, cart) {
  if (user) {
    if (cart.items.length > 0) {
      if (user.isMember) return cart.total * 0.9;
      else return cart.total;
    } else return 0;
  } else return 0;
}

// Guarded
function discount(user, cart) {
  if (!user) return 0;
  if (cart.items.length === 0) return 0;
  return user.isMember ? cart.total * 0.9 : cart.total;
}$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b844042a-70c1-5999-afe4-b82136f22c89', '19f9be40-6bce-5d35-a660-2013f818759c', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$A lookup instead of a chain$c$, $c$When each branch only returns a different value, data beats branching.$c$, $c$const labels = { paid: 'Paid', pending: 'Awaiting payment', failed: 'Payment failed' };

function statusLabel(status) {
  return labels[status] ?? 'Unknown status';
}

console.log(statusLabel('pending'));  // 'Awaiting payment'
console.log(statusLabel('refunded')); // 'Unknown status'$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('48c650d7-68e7-50e7-b8de-f3fb5e9861a9', '19f9be40-6bce-5d35-a660-2013f818759c', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Guarding with `if (!value)` when `0` or `''` are valid inputs; check `value == null` or the specific case instead.
- Returning different *types* from different exits (a number from one, `undefined` from another) and forcing callers to check.
- Turning every conditional into a lookup, even when branches contain real logic.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b0f191fd-b8db-5bdf-8d3e-7a8bb361a2f1', '19f9be40-6bce-5d35-a660-2013f818759c', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$In live coding, interviewers notice whether you validate inputs before the main logic and whether your function reads top to bottom. Saying "I'll guard against empty input first" is a small habit that signals experience.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('c344973a-dbaa-5634-8c7e-eaf00fc6973e', '19f9be40-6bce-5d35-a660-2013f818759c', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Validate a username** is built for guard clauses: several rules, each a reason to return early.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"validate-username"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('f91ab53d-2fc2-50de-ae37-d6e8fcc944e9', '09255bea-ad40-5c4d-b901-2536ec8e82ac', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Three syntaxes, two behaviours$c$, $c$A **function declaration** (`function name() {}`) is hoisted with its body, so it can be called anywhere in its scope, even above the line where it is written. That makes declarations good for top-level helpers.

A **function expression** assigns a function to a variable (`const f = function () {}`). The variable follows normal `const`/`let` rules: it exists only after its line runs, so calling it earlier throws a ReferenceError. Expressions are useful when you want the function to be a value, for example passed as an argument or stored in an object.

An **arrow function** (`const f = () => {}`) is a shorter expression syntax with two important differences: it has no `this` of its own (it uses the `this` of the surrounding code), and it has no `arguments` object and cannot be used with `new`. A single expression body returns its value implicitly; a block body needs an explicit `return`.

Because arrows inherit `this`, they are ideal for callbacks inside methods and awkward as object methods that need `this` to be the object. Choose the syntax by what the function needs, not by fashion.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7488dca7-60e8-5d44-9c43-bc9b2c37c3b1', '09255bea-ad40-5c4d-b901-2536ec8e82ac', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Hoisting in action$c$, $c$The declaration works early; the expression does not exist yet.$c$, $c$console.log(square(4)); // 16

function square(n) {
  return n * n;
}

try {
  console.log(double(4));
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.name); // 'ReferenceError'
}

const double = (n) => n * 2;$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('2dfb80e9-3dc6-5f82-9fd7-750032e14627', '09255bea-ad40-5c4d-b901-2536ec8e82ac', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Implicit return and returning an object$c$, $c$Wrap an object literal in parentheses or the braces are read as a block.$c$, $c$const toLabel = (item) => `${item.name} (${item.qty})`;
const toPoint = (x, y) => ({ x, y });

console.log(toLabel({ name: 'Tea', qty: 2 })); // 'Tea (2)'
console.log(toPoint(1, 2));                     // { x: 1, y: 2 }$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('0bec0f1d-5bc7-5aa0-a722-ef081b910ac5', '09255bea-ad40-5c4d-b901-2536ec8e82ac', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- `const f = () => { x: 1 }` returns `undefined`; the braces are a block, not an object.
- Using an arrow function as an object method and expecting `this` to be the object.
- Relying on hoisting so much that helper definitions end up far below their first use.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('65dd9111-1520-569a-9fba-3e916c66ac13', '09255bea-ad40-5c4d-b901-2536ec8e82ac', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"What is the difference between a function declaration and a function expression?" and "when should you not use an arrow function?" are near-certain questions at junior level. Answer with hoisting and `this`, and give one concrete example of each.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('ac38c08d-a444-55cc-bad9-f23a667fca46', '09255bea-ad40-5c4d-b901-2536ec8e82ac', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Compose a greeting** asks you to build small functions from other functions using all three syntaxes.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"compose-greeting"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('6db8ff2b-e3ac-50c5-85c2-77296064a9a6', 'c241d391-cd31-5d58-b8cf-a88a439b17c6', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Flexible inputs without ceremony$c$, $c$A **default parameter** (`function f(a, b = 10)`) is used when the argument is `undefined`, either because it was omitted or because `undefined` was passed explicitly. `null` does *not* trigger the default; it is a real value. Defaults are evaluated at call time, left to right, so a later default can use an earlier parameter.

A **rest parameter** (`...items`) must be last and collects every remaining argument into a real array. It replaces the old `arguments` object and works in arrow functions too.

**Spread** uses the same three dots on the other side: in a call, `f(...arr)` passes each element as a separate argument; in an array literal, `[...a, ...b]` copies elements into a new array; in an object literal, `{ ...obj, x: 1 }` copies own enumerable properties and lets later keys override earlier ones.

Spread copies are *shallow*: nested objects are shared with the original. That is fine for most updates but matters when you later mutate the nested part.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b138e5c8-39b7-5bd6-a3f3-9e27a110f445', 'c241d391-cd31-5d58-b8cf-a88a439b17c6', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Defaults and rest together$c$, $c$`undefined` triggers the default; `null` does not. The rest parameter is a real array with `length` and array methods.$c$, $c$function total(taxRate = 0.18, ...amounts) {
  const sum = amounts.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, 0);
  return sum * (1 + taxRate);
}

console.log(total(undefined, 100, 50)); // 177
console.log(total(0, 100, 50));         // 150
console.log(total(null, 100));          // 100 * (1 + null) -> 100$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b9cfa9d3-c7bd-5884-8499-c7b961bce987', 'c241d391-cd31-5d58-b8cf-a88a439b17c6', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Spread for copies and overrides$c$, $c$Later keys win, and the original is left untouched.$c$, $c$const base = { theme: 'dark', fontSize: 14 };
const custom = { ...base, fontSize: 16 };
console.log(custom);      // { theme: 'dark', fontSize: 16 }
console.log(base.fontSize); // 14

const nums = [3, 9, 1];
console.log(Math.max(...nums)); // 9$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('f68098d2-9861-5880-874a-73b1c2ce69d2', 'c241d391-cd31-5d58-b8cf-a88a439b17c6', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Expecting a default to apply for `null`.
- Placing a rest parameter anywhere but last (a syntax error).
- Assuming `{ ...obj }` deep-copies nested objects.
- Spreading a very large array into `Math.max(...arr)`, which can exceed the argument limit.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7349dce2-18ba-5b29-bc1a-69cf93a769f5', 'c241d391-cd31-5d58-b8cf-a88a439b17c6', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Expect "what is the difference between rest and spread?" and a snippet mixing `undefined` and `null` with defaults. The precise answer about `undefined` (not `null`) triggering defaults separates candidates who have read the rules from those who guess.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d3a3f2b1-1ebe-5c75-a69b-b1ce962b40a1', 'c241d391-cd31-5d58-b8cf-a88a439b17c6', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Average of arguments** uses a rest parameter and a default to handle any number of inputs.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"average-of-arguments"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('70ae1171-02f3-55c8-bb5c-b17a81f98512', '205ecf11-4517-510e-a008-3443cddd5230', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Functions are values$c$, $c$In JavaScript a function is an object, so it can be stored in a variable, placed in an array, passed to another function, or returned from one. A function that takes or returns a function is a **higher-order function**. `Array.prototype.map`, `filter`, `addEventListener`, and `setTimeout` all take callbacks; `bind` returns a new function.

A **callback** is simply the function you pass to be called later. Some callbacks run immediately and synchronously (`map` calls yours for each element before returning), while others run after an event or delay (`setTimeout`, `addEventListener`). Knowing which is which is essential for predicting output order.

Returning a function is how you build small **factories**: call `makeMultiplier(3)` once and get back a function that remembers `3` through its closure. This pattern is the foundation of currying, memoisation, and most utility libraries.

Write higher-order code so that the reader can see the shape: name the callback parameter for what it receives, keep callbacks short, and avoid nesting more than two levels deep.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('51e9cc8e-d87f-576e-9069-54c303c1efe6', '205ecf11-4517-510e-a008-3443cddd5230', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Passing behaviour$c$, $c$`applyTwice` does not care what `fn` does; it only knows how to call it.$c$, $c$function applyTwice(fn, value) {
  return fn(fn(value));
}

const addFive = (n) => n + 5;
const shout = (s) => s.toUpperCase() + '!';

console.log(applyTwice(addFive, 10));  // 20
console.log(applyTwice(shout, 'go')); // 'GO!!'$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('80eb8ead-61ff-559a-8d0c-22ce7eb2c863', '205ecf11-4517-510e-a008-3443cddd5230', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Returning a configured function$c$, $c$Each returned function keeps its own `factor` through closure.$c$, $c$function makeMultiplier(factor) {
  return (n) => n * factor;
}

const double = makeMultiplier(2);
const triple = makeMultiplier(3);

console.log(double(7)); // 14
console.log(triple(7)); // 21
console.log([1, 2, 3].map(double)); // [2, 4, 6]$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('a9655ad2-9007-5feb-8dcb-62f42765ab4c', '205ecf11-4517-510e-a008-3443cddd5230', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Calling the callback when passing it: `setTimeout(tick(), 1000)` passes `tick`'s *result*, not `tick`.
- Assuming every callback is asynchronous; `map` and `forEach` run yours synchronously.
- Building callback pyramids instead of extracting named functions or using promises.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('4baabdec-d163-5d3e-92b4-fc10fe9c3d6d', '205ecf11-4517-510e-a008-3443cddd5230', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"What is a higher-order function?" is a standard warm-up; a stronger answer names built-ins and writes `makeMultiplier` from memory. Output-order questions often hinge on whether a callback runs synchronously.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('147e7057-55e0-554e-b330-4383c1e25bcd', '205ecf11-4517-510e-a008-3443cddd5230', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Make a multiplier** asks for exactly the factory pattern above, then extends it.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"make-multiplier"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('24230ccb-eaab-50c3-bfb8-3cf27250e177', '205ecf11-4517-510e-a008-3443cddd5230', 7, $c$summary$c$, $c$Key takeaways$c$, $c$Functions are values: you can pass them in, return them, and store them. A higher-order function does at least one of those. Callbacks passed to array methods run synchronously and immediately; callbacks passed to timers and event listeners run later. Factories such as makeMultiplier return functions that remember their configuration through closure, which is the pattern behind currying, memoisation, and most utility libraries you will use at work.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('5d817510-418d-51c3-90f2-11c5c7ba987b', 'b8780e8e-11f1-57bb-88ca-98409b4ce5f7', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Keys, values and access$c$, $c$An object literal is a comma-separated list of `key: value` pairs inside braces. Keys are strings (or symbols); if you write a number or an identifier as a key it is converted to a string. When a variable has the same name as the key you can use **shorthand** (`{ name, age }`), and a key computed at runtime goes in square brackets: `{ [`item-${id}`]: value }`.

**Dot notation** (`user.name`) works when the key is a valid identifier that you know while writing the code. **Bracket notation** (`user['first name']`, `user[key]`) is required when the key contains spaces or other characters, or when it lives in a variable.

Reading a key that does not exist gives `undefined`, and so does reading a key that was explicitly set to `undefined`. To tell them apart use `'key' in obj` or `Object.hasOwn(obj, 'key')`. `delete obj.key` removes a property entirely.

`Object.keys`, `Object.values`, and `Object.entries` turn an object into arrays so you can loop, filter, or map over its contents.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('1540d780-d0fc-564d-a9c5-ab93523b6a1d', 'b8780e8e-11f1-57bb-88ca-98409b4ce5f7', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Shorthand, computed keys and brackets$c$, $c$The key stored in `field` decides which property is read.$c$, $c$const name = 'Asha';
const age = 29;
const field = 'age';

const person = { name, age, ['id-' + 7]: true };

console.log(person[field]);   // 29
console.log(person['id-7']);  // true
console.log(Object.keys(person)); // ['name', 'age', 'id-7']$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('21b8f165-823b-5cb5-ab72-5ce8624acc41', 'b8780e8e-11f1-57bb-88ca-98409b4ce5f7', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Missing versus undefined$c$, $c$`in` reports presence of the key, not the truthiness of its value.$c$, $c$const settings = { theme: undefined };

console.log(settings.theme);        // undefined
console.log(settings.language);     // undefined
console.log('theme' in settings);    // true
console.log('language' in settings); // false

delete settings.theme;
console.log('theme' in settings);    // false$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('ce783378-1241-5e5b-aecf-5c6f54ad3662', 'b8780e8e-11f1-57bb-88ca-98409b4ce5f7', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- `obj.key` when `key` is a variable; that looks up a property literally named `key`.
- Assuming numeric keys stay numbers: `Object.keys({ 1: 'a' })` gives `['1']`.
- Using `obj.key === undefined` to detect a missing key.
- Comparing two objects with `===` and expecting equal contents to be equal.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('11c91141-852d-58c8-926f-ce10f545b0fe', 'b8780e8e-11f1-57bb-88ca-98409b4ce5f7', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Counting occurrences with an object as a map is a classic first task. Interviewers also like "how do you check whether an object has a property?" and expect `in`, `Object.hasOwn`, and the difference from reading `undefined`.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('391ff42d-a2d8-52f3-bc51-bc115aff7b81', 'b8780e8e-11f1-57bb-88ca-98409b4ce5f7', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Count words** uses an object as a tally and needs bracket notation throughout.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"count-words"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('902a40e0-c8c7-54be-894b-c6b4442e5eaa', 'b8780e8e-11f1-57bb-88ca-98409b4ce5f7', 7, $c$summary$c$, $c$Key takeaways$c$, $c$Object keys are strings (or symbols); values can be anything. Use dot notation for fixed names you know while writing code and bracket notation for dynamic or unusual keys. Reading a missing key gives undefined, so use the in operator or Object.hasOwn when you need to know whether a key exists. Object.keys, values, and entries convert an object into arrays you can loop over, and objects compare by reference, never by content.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('0556d3a2-826f-5439-ba33-2423679a0649', '4c93d3c7-ad37-5638-b596-1097d5f52e5b', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$this is decided by the call, not the definition$c$, $c$A **method** is a function stored as a property. Method shorthand (`greet() { … }`) is the usual way to write one. Inside a regular function, `this` is bound when the function is *called*: if it is called as `obj.method()`, `this` is `obj`. If the same function is called on its own, `this` is `undefined` in strict mode (modules and classes are always strict).

That single rule explains the classic bugs. Assigning `const g = obj.greet` and calling `g()` loses the object. Passing `obj.greet` as a callback to `setTimeout` or an event listener does the same, because the caller invokes it without an object.

**Arrow functions** do not have their own `this`; they use the `this` of the surrounding scope at the time they were created. That is why an arrow callback inside a method still sees the object, and why an arrow used *as* a method does not.

When you need to force a particular `this`, `fn.call(obj, …)` and `fn.apply(obj, […])` invoke immediately, and `fn.bind(obj)` returns a new function permanently bound to `obj`.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('a4ad1588-c5c7-54a3-8486-8e27956dfd9e', '4c93d3c7-ad37-5638-b596-1097d5f52e5b', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Losing and keeping this$c$, $c$The detached call has no object; `bind` restores one.$c$, $c$const counter = {
  count: 0,
  increment() {
    this.count += 1;
    return this.count;
  },
};

console.log(counter.increment()); // 1

const detached = counter.increment;
try {
  detached();
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.name); // 'TypeError' (this is undefined)
}

const bound = counter.increment.bind(counter);
console.log(bound()); // 2$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('f0952997-4166-5561-bde7-74857ac11773', '4c93d3c7-ad37-5638-b596-1097d5f52e5b', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Arrow callbacks inside methods$c$, $c$The arrow keeps the method's `this`; a regular function callback would not.$c$, $c$const cart = {
  items: [10, 20],
  taxRate: 0.1,
  totals() {
    return this.items.map((price) => price * (1 + this.taxRate));
  },
};

console.log(cart.totals()); // [11, 22]$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('af5f681b-8313-53fc-8c93-86b8f20dfb23', '4c93d3c7-ad37-5638-b596-1097d5f52e5b', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Defining a method as an arrow function and expecting `this` to be the object.
- Passing `obj.method` directly as a callback without binding.
- Believing `this` is "the function" or "where it was written" rather than how it was called.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('ea185b97-486a-518f-85dd-2a0bf2d0d3d2', '4c93d3c7-ad37-5638-b596-1097d5f52e5b', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$`this` is one of the most frequent junior and mid-level topics. A reliable answer: "For regular functions, `this` is set by the call site; arrows inherit it lexically; call/apply/bind override it." Then walk through the detached-method example.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('74496920-f7dd-5b95-ab45-d34a68a17dc4', '4c93d3c7-ad37-5638-b596-1097d5f52e5b', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Bank account object** has methods that must share state through `this` and survive being passed around.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"bank-account-object"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('9e7387dc-b248-5cda-93a2-1290af7a4766', '7b94df60-e427-53dc-9063-12150ce05e4c', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Unpacking and safe access$c$, $c$**Object destructuring** matches keys: `const { name, age } = person`. Rename with a colon (`{ name: fullName }`), supply a default with `=` (`{ role = 'viewer' }`), and combine both (`{ role: userRole = 'viewer' }`). Nested patterns unpack deeper levels, and a rest element (`...others`) collects the remaining keys into a new object.

**Array destructuring** matches positions: `const [first, , third] = list` skips the second item, and `[head, ...tail]` splits a list. Swapping two variables is a one-liner: `[a, b] = [b, a]`.

Destructuring works in function parameters, which is the idiomatic way to accept an options object with defaults: `function connect({ host = 'localhost', port = 5432 } = {})`. The trailing `= {}` protects against calling the function with no argument at all.

**Optional chaining** `a?.b` returns `undefined` instead of throwing when `a` is `null` or `undefined`; it also works for calls (`fn?.()`) and brackets (`obj?.[key]`). Pair it with the **nullish coalescing** operator `??` to supply a fallback only for `null`/`undefined`, unlike `||`, which also replaces `0`, `''`, and `false`.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('58c3602d-9aec-51b5-82a9-d22108bea714', '7b94df60-e427-53dc-9063-12150ce05e4c', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Rename, default, nest, rest$c$, $c$Every feature in one pattern.$c$, $c$const order = {
  id: 42,
  customer: { name: 'Ravi', address: { city: 'Pune' } },
  items: ['tea', 'biscuits'],
};

const {
  id: orderId,
  customer: { name, address: { city, country = 'IN' } },
  ...rest
} = order;

console.log(orderId, name, city, country); // 42 'Ravi' 'Pune' 'IN'
console.log(Object.keys(rest));            // ['items']

const [firstItem, ...others] = order.items;
console.log(firstItem, others);            // 'tea' ['biscuits']$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('61892e67-bc8d-5170-8fd2-8945161a4c84', '7b94df60-e427-53dc-9063-12150ce05e4c', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Optional chaining with a fallback$c$, $c$`??` keeps a legitimate `0`; `||` would have replaced it.$c$, $c$const profile = { stats: { streak: 0 } };

console.log(profile.stats?.streak ?? 'no data');   // 0
console.log(profile.stats?.streak || 'no data');   // 'no data'
console.log(profile.badges?.length ?? 0);          // 0
console.log(profile.badges?.[0]);                  // undefined (no throw)$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('30e6be14-68c0-573f-a121-6ac10a4163cb', '7b94df60-e427-53dc-9063-12150ce05e4c', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Destructuring from `undefined` (for example a missing options argument) throws; add `= {}`.
- Confusing rename syntax: `{ name: fullName }` reads `name` *into* `fullName`, not the other way round.
- Using `||` for defaults and silently replacing `0` or `''`.
- Sprinkling `?.` everywhere, which hides real bugs where the value should never be missing.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('2df4a0c7-3422-584d-b56c-b44144be0817', '7b94df60-e427-53dc-9063-12150ce05e4c', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Expect a snippet mixing `?.`, `??`, and `||` and a request to explain each output. Destructuring in parameters is also a common "clean this code up" refactor in live sessions.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('51ec3021-6e7a-5de7-af18-c6d1d13ee242', '7b94df60-e427-53dc-9063-12150ce05e4c', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Extract an address** combines nested destructuring with defaults and optional chaining on incomplete records.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"extract-address"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('59c5c75b-90f1-52dc-b38f-162a4f9e4298', '2048fd9b-9528-5f39-9fcb-c26caa3e8ddb', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Arrays are ordered, resizable, and mutable$c$, $c$An array literal `[1, 2, 3]` creates an ordered list with a `length` that grows and shrinks automatically. Indexes start at 0, and reading past the end returns `undefined` rather than throwing.

`push` and `pop` add and remove at the end (fast); `unshift` and `shift` do the same at the start (slower, because every index moves). `splice(start, deleteCount, ...items)` removes and/or inserts anywhere and returns the removed items. `sort` and `reverse` reorder in place. All of these **mutate** the array you call them on.

Other methods return a **new** array and leave the original alone: `slice`, `concat`, `map`, `filter`, and the newer `toSorted`, `toReversed`, `toSpliced`, and `with`. Spread (`[...arr]`) and `Array.from(arr)` make shallow copies.

The distinction matters because arrays are shared by reference. If two variables point to the same array, mutating through one changes what the other sees. When a function receives an array it should not change, copy first or use a non-mutating method.

`Array.isArray(value)` is the reliable way to check for an array; `typeof [] === 'object'` is not helpful.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('bc72db32-c24d-5ce6-83a7-ba3b38682cb7', '2048fd9b-9528-5f39-9fcb-c26caa3e8ddb', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Ends and middles$c$, $c$`splice` both removes and inserts. Note what each call returns.$c$, $c$const queue = ['a', 'b', 'c'];

queue.push('d');            // returns 4 (new length)
const first = queue.shift(); // 'a'
console.log(queue);         // ['b', 'c', 'd']

const removed = queue.splice(1, 1, 'x', 'y');
console.log(removed);       // ['c']
console.log(queue);         // ['b', 'x', 'y', 'd']$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8baf74f8-eea7-5b91-b795-4c1fea1ab67c', '2048fd9b-9528-5f39-9fcb-c26caa3e8ddb', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Mutating versus copying$c$, $c$`sort` changed `scores`; `toSorted` left `names` alone.$c$, $c$const scores = [40, 9, 100];
scores.sort();
console.log(scores); // [100, 40, 9]  (string order!)

const names = ['Zed', 'amy', 'Bo'];
const sorted = names.toSorted((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));
console.log(names);  // ['Zed', 'amy', 'Bo']
console.log(sorted); // ['amy', 'Bo', 'Zed']$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b5c48fc2-39af-58a8-8863-352a22c31106', '2048fd9b-9528-5f39-9fcb-c26caa3e8ddb', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Expecting `push` to return the array (it returns the new length).
- Calling `sort()` on numbers without a comparator and getting lexicographic order.
- Mutating an array received as an argument and surprising the caller.
- Using `delete arr[i]`, which leaves a hole instead of removing the slot.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('bda2082b-3c8d-5b2b-9116-ab5b0236a766', '2048fd9b-9528-5f39-9fcb-c26caa3e8ddb', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"Which array methods mutate?" is a quick screening question. Live-coding tasks often silently test whether you avoid mutating input, so mention it: "I'll copy the array so the original stays intact."$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('2663ca0f-2066-5204-ba24-5840cb2a8708', '2048fd9b-9528-5f39-9fcb-c26caa3e8ddb', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Queue simulation** is all `push`, `shift`, and `splice`, with a twist about returning the removed items.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"queue-simulation"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('a9358e6f-8bfc-5da3-b57c-4d571ac5b3b6', '5f7ff923-715e-5b93-8edc-224dfa05c89e', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Same length, fewer items, one value$c$, $c$`map(fn)` calls `fn` on every item and returns a **new array of the same length** holding the results. Use it when you want to convert each item, for example objects to labels.

`filter(fn)` returns a **new array with only the items** for which `fn` returned a truthy value. The items themselves are not changed.

`reduce(fn, initial)` walks the array carrying an accumulator: `fn(accumulator, item)` returns the next accumulator, and the final one is the result. It can produce **anything**: a number, an object used as a tally, or even another array. Always pass an initial value; without it, `reduce` uses the first item as the accumulator, which breaks on empty arrays and often yields the wrong type.

All three take callbacks with the signature `(item, index, array)` and do not mutate the original. They run synchronously and return before the next line executes.

Chaining reads well when each step is simple: `orders.filter(isPaid).map(toTotal).reduce(sum, 0)`. When a chain gets long or does several passes over large data, a single loop or a single `reduce` may be clearer and faster, but clarity should win at beginner level.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('02de64a8-64c7-5063-abc2-416c1cc81fee', '5f7ff923-715e-5b93-8edc-224dfa05c89e', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Three shapes$c$, $c$Same input, three kinds of output.$c$, $c$const items = [
  { name: 'Tea', price: 40, qty: 2 },
  { name: 'Cake', price: 120, qty: 1 },
  { name: 'Water', price: 20, qty: 0 },
];

const names = items.map((item) => item.name);
const inCart = items.filter((item) => item.qty > 0);
const total = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.qty, 0);

console.log(names);           // ['Tea', 'Cake', 'Water']
console.log(inCart.length);   // 2
console.log(total);           // 200$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7392c1f0-9e56-5b77-b76b-f23e778df3e0', '5f7ff923-715e-5b93-8edc-224dfa05c89e', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$reduce as a tally$c$, $c$The accumulator is an object; the initial value `{}` is essential.$c$, $c$const votes = ['tea', 'coffee', 'tea', 'juice', 'tea'];

const tally = votes.reduce((counts, drink) => {
  counts[drink] = (counts[drink] ?? 0) + 1;
  return counts;
}, {});

console.log(tally); // { tea: 3, coffee: 1, juice: 1 }$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('2dfceb70-94a6-5154-9235-9d70f985c9c5', '5f7ff923-715e-5b93-8edc-224dfa05c89e', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Forgetting to `return` from the reduce callback, so the accumulator becomes `undefined` on the second step.
- Omitting the initial value and crashing on an empty array.
- Using `map` for side effects and discarding the result (use `forEach` or a loop).
- Using `filter` then `[0]` when `find` says what you mean.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('50422207-fae6-5f20-8f5f-a43f1bfb5966', '5f7ff923-715e-5b93-8edc-224dfa05c89e', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Almost every frontend interview includes one of these. Common asks: implement `map` with `reduce`, group objects by a key, or explain why `reduce` needs an initial value. Be ready to write the tally example from memory.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('bc3f10a6-1660-5a1c-af54-56c93d979a0c', '5f7ff923-715e-5b93-8edc-224dfa05c89e', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Cart summary** chains all three on a realistic order list.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"cart-summary"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('c7ac0a5a-1a6e-57a4-a67a-b4354737446d', '02ff09af-a841-545c-ae66-5cd811d3b0bf', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Ask the right question$c$, $c$The search methods differ in what they return. `includes(value)` answers a yes/no question for a primitive using strict equality (and correctly finds `NaN`). `indexOf` returns the position or `-1`. `find(fn)` returns the **first item** whose callback is truthy (or `undefined`), while `findIndex` returns its position. `some(fn)` is true if **any** item matches; `every(fn)` is true only if **all** match (and is true for an empty array).

`sort(comparator)` orders in place. The comparator receives two items and returns a negative number to put the first earlier, positive to put it later, or zero to keep them equal. Without a comparator, items are converted to strings, which is why `[10, 9, 1].sort()` gives `[1, 10, 9]`. For numbers use `(a, b) => a - b`; for strings use `a.localeCompare(b)`. Modern engines guarantee a **stable** sort, so equal items keep their original order, which lets you sort by one key and then another.

**Immutability** means treating arrays as values you replace rather than change. Instead of `arr.push(x)`, produce `[...arr, x]`; instead of `arr[i] = v`, use `arr.with(i, v)`; instead of `sort`, use `toSorted`. The pay-off is that comparing old and new arrays by reference tells you whether anything changed, which UI frameworks rely on to skip re-rendering.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('806a404c-df9f-5a1b-aeef-5eda9fc1dbbf', '02ff09af-a841-545c-ae66-5cd811d3b0bf', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Search methods compared$c$, $c$Each method answers a different question about the same data.$c$, $c$const users = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Mira', active: false },
  { id: 2, name: 'Dev', active: true },
];

console.log(users.find((u) => u.active)?.name);        // 'Dev'
console.log(users.findIndex((u) => u.id === 1));        // 0
console.log(users.some((u) => u.active));               // true
console.log(users.every((u) => u.active));              // false
console.log([1, NaN].includes(NaN), [1, NaN].indexOf(NaN)); // true -1$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d1ab3fc7-4937-5217-a564-06b9dab84f8b', '02ff09af-a841-545c-ae66-5cd811d3b0bf', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Comparators and non-mutating updates$c$, $c$Sort by score descending, then by name as a tie-breaker, without touching the original.$c$, $c$const results = [
  { name: 'Bo', score: 80 },
  { name: 'Al', score: 95 },
  { name: 'Cy', score: 80 },
];

const ranked = results.toSorted((a, b) => b.score - a.score || a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
console.log(ranked.map((r) => r.name)); // ['Al', 'Bo', 'Cy']

const updated = ranked.with(0, { ...ranked[0], score: 100 });
console.log(ranked[0].score, updated[0].score); // 95 100$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('79fb0910-4ba9-53f2-8618-9f7f884d4584', '02ff09af-a841-545c-ae66-5cd811d3b0bf', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- `sort()` without a comparator on numbers.
- Expecting `includes` to find objects by content; it compares references.
- Returning `true`/`false` from a comparator instead of a number.
- Reassigning inside `sort`'s callback or mutating items while sorting.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('da6ac275-0169-5b0d-a6e4-794bb6b547aa', '02ff09af-a841-545c-ae66-5cd811d3b0bf', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Sorting by multiple keys and "why does [10, 9, 1].sort() give [1, 10, 9]?" are common. Explaining why immutable updates help React-style rendering is a bonus that signals real-world experience.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('fe7295bb-6516-51f3-8750-897e33645036', '02ff09af-a841-545c-ae66-5cd811d3b0bf', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Top scores** needs a multi-key comparator and must not mutate the input.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"top-scores"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8d52e32b-1d6a-59b2-84ae-32a491c401b3', 'b5bda456-b413-5fe8-9864-5087df3974bd', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Immutable text and the methods that matter$c$, $c$Strings are primitive and **immutable**: no method changes the original; each returns a new string. `text.length` counts UTF-16 code units, which is why some emoji report a length of 2.

**Template literals** use backticks. They interpolate any expression with `${…}`, keep line breaks as written, and avoid the quote-escaping dance of concatenation. They are the default choice for building text.

The everyday toolkit: `slice(start, end)` extracts a range (negative indexes count from the end); `split(separator)` turns a string into an array and `join(separator)` does the reverse; `trim` removes surrounding whitespace; `toUpperCase`/`toLowerCase` change case; `includes`, `startsWith`, and `endsWith` answer yes/no questions; `indexOf` gives a position; `replace` changes the first match and `replaceAll` changes every match; `padStart` is handy for zero-padding; `at(-1)` reads the last character.

Compare strings with `===`. For user-facing ordering use `localeCompare`, which understands accents and case the way people expect.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8471a32b-2bd1-5d9a-a9ad-77fe76aba51e', 'b5bda456-b413-5fe8-9864-5087df3974bd', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Template literals and the toolkit$c$, $c$Interpolation, a method chain, and `split`/`join` to rebuild text.$c$, $c$const name = '  Priya Nair ';
const order = 3;

const clean = name.trim();
console.log(`Hi ${clean}, this is order #${String(order).padStart(3, '0')}`);
// 'Hi Priya Nair, this is order #003'

const initials = clean.split(' ').map((part) => part[0]).join('');
console.log(initials);                 // 'PN'
console.log(clean.slice(-4));           // 'Nair'
console.log('a-b-c'.replaceAll('-', '+')); // 'a+b+c'$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('227811aa-7f99-5974-aa9a-ee7609bb7f58', 'b5bda456-b413-5fe8-9864-5087df3974bd', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Immutability in practice$c$, $c$`toUpperCase` did not change `word`; the result had to be captured.$c$, $c$const word = 'quiet';
word.toUpperCase();
console.log(word);          // 'quiet'

const loud = word.toUpperCase();
console.log(loud);          // 'QUIET'
console.log(word.at(-1));   // 't'$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('5b99cf81-f6d7-57dc-8d13-48d707dc3ac2', 'b5bda456-b413-5fe8-9864-5087df3974bd', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Calling a method and ignoring its return value, expecting the string to change.
- `replace` when every occurrence should change; use `replaceAll` or a global regex.
- Comparing case-different strings with `===` without normalising case first.
- Building long strings with `+` inside loops when `join` on an array is clearer.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('0d5ca7c4-863c-5246-a488-cb4327d2258a', 'b5bda456-b413-5fe8-9864-5087df3974bd', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Reverse a string, check a palindrome, count vowels, title-case a sentence: string manipulation is the most common warm-up. Knowing `split`/`join`/`slice` cold and stating that strings are immutable gets you through quickly.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('6055194a-f731-5949-bd05-ab7e6132f901', 'b5bda456-b413-5fe8-9864-5087df3974bd', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Title case** exercises `split`, `map`, `slice`, and `join` together.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"title-case"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b5809af3-ba6a-5e0c-8a42-ea4e7872af94', 'b5bda456-b413-5fe8-9864-5087df3974bd', 7, $c$summary$c$, $c$Key takeaways$c$, $c$Strings never change; every method hands back a new string, so capture the result. Template literals with ${} are the default way to build text from values and keep line breaks. The methods you will reach for most are slice, split and join, trim, includes and startsWith, replace and replaceAll, padStart, and at. Compare with === and, for user-facing ordering, localeCompare.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('07628d4f-161d-5524-8060-5a066891d588', '4042458f-b05c-58ad-b783-becde2dccd8b', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$One number type, many surprises$c$, $c$JavaScript has a single `number` type: a 64-bit IEEE 754 float. Integers up to `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` (2^53 − 1) are exact; beyond that, precision is lost, which is what `BigInt` is for. Decimal fractions like 0.1 cannot be represented exactly, so `0.1 + 0.2` evaluates to `0.30000000000000004`. For money, work in the smallest unit (paise, cents) as integers, or round only when displaying with `toFixed(2)`, and compare with a tolerance (`Math.abs(a - b) < Number.EPSILON`) rather than `===`.

`Number(text)` converts the whole string and gives `NaN` if any part is invalid; `parseInt(text, 10)` and `parseFloat(text)` read as many leading digits as they can and stop, so `parseInt('42px', 10)` is `42`. Always pass the radix to `parseInt`.

`NaN` ("not a number") is the result of invalid arithmetic. It is the only value not equal to itself, so `x === NaN` is always false; use `Number.isNaN(x)`. Any arithmetic with `NaN` produces `NaN`, so catch it early.

The `Math` helpers you will use constantly: `round`, `floor`, `ceil`, `trunc`, `max`, `min`, `abs`, and `random` (a float in `[0, 1)`). A random integer between `min` and `max` inclusive is `Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min`.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('e5c93735-c614-5632-9916-0415e320ff31', '4042458f-b05c-58ad-b783-becde2dccd8b', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Rounding and money$c$, $c$Store paise as integers; format only at the edge.$c$, $c$console.log(0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3);                     // false
console.log(Math.abs(0.1 + 0.2 - 0.3) < Number.EPSILON); // true

const paise = 12345; // ₹123.45 stored as an integer
console.log((paise / 100).toFixed(2));               // '123.45'
console.log((1.005).toFixed(2));                     // '1.00' (binary rounding!)
console.log(Math.round(1.005 * 100) / 100);          // 1 (same reason)$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('1e13bd08-78ce-5f57-806a-3ca8c131c4cd', '4042458f-b05c-58ad-b783-becde2dccd8b', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Parsing and NaN$c$, $c$`Number` is strict; `parseInt` is forgiving. `NaN` needs `Number.isNaN`.$c$, $c$console.log(Number('42px'), parseInt('42px', 10)); // NaN 42
console.log(Number(''), Number('  7 '));           // 0 7
console.log(parseInt('08', 10), parseFloat('3.9kg')); // 8 3.9

const bad = Number('abc');
console.log(bad === NaN, Number.isNaN(bad));        // false true
console.log(Number.isNaN('abc'), isNaN('abc'));     // false true (global isNaN coerces)$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('e068efa0-8990-513f-a4c9-376cefd75384', '4042458f-b05c-58ad-b783-becde2dccd8b', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Comparing floats with `===` after arithmetic.
- `parseInt` without a radix, or on a float expecting rounding.
- Using the global `isNaN`, which converts first and reports `true` for `'abc'`.
- Storing money as decimals and accumulating rounding errors.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('c2bb4ffe-a689-50a2-8379-15191f87adda', '4042458f-b05c-58ad-b783-becde2dccd8b', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"Why is 0.1 + 0.2 !== 0.3?" and "how do you check for NaN?" are staples. A confident answer mentions IEEE 754, integers for money, and `Number.isNaN`.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('39ee09e6-738b-58b3-a697-9af74c15bfaa', '4042458f-b05c-58ad-b783-becde2dccd8b', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Format currency** takes an integer amount of paise and formats it as rupees with grouping.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"format-currency"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('5858ffcf-45fe-5e48-9a37-4c58c985206d', 'fb2c998e-4aeb-5168-a639-07adc3699d0d', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Text in, objects out$c$, $c$JSON is a text format for data: objects, arrays, strings, finite numbers, booleans, and `null`. `JSON.stringify(value)` turns a JavaScript value into that text; `JSON.parse(text)` turns text back into a value. Every network request, `localStorage` entry, and config file goes through this pair.

`stringify` has rules worth memorising. `undefined`, functions, and symbols are **omitted** from objects and become `null` inside arrays. `NaN` and `Infinity` become `null`. `Date` objects become ISO strings (their `toJSON` result), and they come back from `parse` as plain strings, not dates. `Map` and `Set` become `{}`. Keys are always strings. Circular references throw.

The optional second argument is a **replacer** (an array of keys to keep, or a function), and the third is indentation for readable output: `JSON.stringify(obj, null, 2)`. `parse` accepts a **reviver** function to transform values as they are read, which is how you turn date strings back into `Date` objects.

`JSON.parse` throws a `SyntaxError` on malformed input, so any text you did not create yourself, from a server, a file, or storage, belongs inside `try/catch` with a sensible fallback. A round trip through stringify/parse is a quick deep copy for JSON-safe data, but `structuredClone` is the proper tool because it keeps dates and nested types.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('866a3346-e2c9-52b5-a87d-7a955fc8a916', 'fb2c998e-4aeb-5168-a639-07adc3699d0d', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$What survives a round trip$c$, $c$Watch which values disappear or change.$c$, $c$const original = {
  name: 'Report',
  createdAt: new Date('2026-01-15T00:00:00Z'),
  tags: ['a', undefined, 'b'],
  notes: undefined,
  ratio: NaN,
  render() { return 'x'; },
};

const text = JSON.stringify(original);
console.log(text);
// {"name":"Report","createdAt":"2026-01-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["a",null,"b"],"ratio":null}

const copy = JSON.parse(text);
console.log(typeof copy.createdAt); // 'string'
console.log('notes' in copy);       // false$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('9317b864-831c-584e-ba91-3d71e71523f9', 'fb2c998e-4aeb-5168-a639-07adc3699d0d', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Parsing safely with a reviver$c$, $c$Malformed input is caught; date strings become dates on the way in.$c$, $c$function parseReport(text) {
  try {
    return JSON.parse(text, (key, value) =>
      key === 'createdAt' ? new Date(value) : value
    );
  } catch {
    return null;
  }
}

console.log(parseReport('{"createdAt":"2026-01-15T00:00:00.000Z"}').createdAt instanceof Date); // true
console.log(parseReport('{not json'));  // null$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('256a0699-13ed-5bc9-a710-70103860b6ba', 'fb2c998e-4aeb-5168-a639-07adc3699d0d', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Expecting dates, `Map`s, or class instances to survive a round trip.
- Calling `JSON.parse` on data from the network or storage without `try/catch`.
- Using stringify/parse as a general deep clone and losing `undefined` values and dates.
- Forgetting that keys become strings, so `{ 1: 'a' }` comes back as `{ '1': 'a' }`.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('a893bbc4-b2a6-54d0-b96d-7d3e3b6970a3', 'fb2c998e-4aeb-5168-a639-07adc3699d0d', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"What does JSON.stringify do with undefined / functions / dates?" is a favourite quick check. Mentioning replacer and reviver, and `structuredClone` for real deep copies, marks you as careful.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7f0d7e1e-3a73-52d3-b438-23693335a4d5', 'fb2c998e-4aeb-5168-a639-07adc3699d0d', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Safe JSON parse** builds the wrapper with a fallback and a reviver.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"safe-json-parse"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('006f9b16-0ea3-53f4-ada3-31b9a0e6e500', '69a7726e-a7f0-55a5-9a0d-49db329eafe6', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Errors are values that travel$c$, $c$When code cannot continue sensibly it should `throw`. Always throw an `Error` (or a subclass such as `TypeError`, `RangeError`, or your own `class ValidationError extends Error`), never a bare string: `Error` objects carry a `name`, a `message`, and a `stack`, and tools rely on those. Since ES2022 you can attach the original problem with `new Error('Could not save', { cause: err })`.

A thrown error unwinds the call stack until it reaches a `try` block with a matching `catch`. Code after the throw in each function does not run. If nothing catches it, the script reports an uncaught exception. `catch (error)` receives the thrown value; the binding is optional when you do not need it.

`finally` runs whether the `try` succeeded, threw, or returned early, which makes it the right place for cleanup: closing a connection, hiding a spinner, releasing a lock.

Catch narrowly. A `catch` that swallows every error and does nothing hides bugs. Inspect `error instanceof SomeError` or `error.name`, handle what you understand, and rethrow the rest. Inside `async` functions the same syntax works with `await`, and a rejected promise behaves exactly like a thrown error.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('5e170dbd-c456-54c7-8c88-cdfa9996fea2', '69a7726e-a7f0-55a5-9a0d-49db329eafe6', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Throwing, catching, and finally$c$, $c$The spinner is hidden on both paths; only the invalid input path logs an error.$c$, $c$class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';
  }
}

function parseQuantity(input) {
  const n = Number(input);
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 1) {
    throw new ValidationError(`Invalid quantity: ${input}`);
  }
  return n;
}

for (const input of ['3', 'many']) {
  try {
    console.log('parsed', parseQuantity(input));
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof ValidationError) console.log(error.name, '-', error.message);
    else throw error;
  } finally {
    console.log('done with', input);
  }
}$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d6e3132a-20df-5721-ae16-ec9a6aa2af80', '69a7726e-a7f0-55a5-9a0d-49db329eafe6', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Wrapping with cause$c$, $c$The outer error explains the situation; `cause` preserves the original detail.$c$, $c$function loadSettings(text) {
  try {
    return JSON.parse(text);
  } catch (error) {
    throw new Error('Settings file is corrupt', { cause: error });
  }
}

try {
  loadSettings('{oops');
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.message);       // 'Settings file is corrupt'
  console.log(error.cause.name);    // 'SyntaxError'
}$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('edddf8e0-dd6e-5ee4-88f0-0ed55c692a38', '69a7726e-a7f0-55a5-9a0d-49db329eafe6', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- `throw 'something went wrong'` (no stack, no name, breaks `instanceof`).
- Empty `catch {}` blocks that silence failures.
- Expecting `try/catch` to catch an error thrown later inside a callback or a promise that was not awaited.
- Doing the cleanup in `try` and skipping it when an error occurs.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('796b2ab4-02bb-5775-9d21-4113b0e18da6', '69a7726e-a7f0-55a5-9a0d-49db329eafe6', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"When does finally run?" and "how do you create a custom error?" are common. Interviewers also probe whether `try/catch` around `setTimeout(() => { throw … })` catches anything (it does not).$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('5e6297ee-cbdb-5247-9137-1d135719a281', '69a7726e-a7f0-55a5-9a0d-49db329eafe6', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Parse age** throws typed errors for bad input and returns a clean value otherwise.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"parse-age"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('ceb3d05f-e764-5218-a4f1-ebbcb304fa9c', '5d5d77c3-801f-5824-a943-8c9c2c102a71', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Make the program show you$c$, $c$A **stack trace** lists the functions that were active when an error was thrown, most recent first. Read the top frame for *where* it failed, then walk down to see *how the code got there*. Framework and library frames can be ignored at first; look for the first frame in your own files.

The console does more than `log`. `console.error` and `console.warn` colour and filter differently. `console.table(arrayOfObjects)` prints a grid. `console.group(label)` / `console.groupEnd()` indents related output. `console.time(label)` / `console.timeEnd(label)` measure elapsed time. `console.assert(condition, message)` prints only when the condition is false. Logging an object shows a *live* reference, so expand it immediately or log `structuredClone(obj)` if it changes later.

**Breakpoints** stop execution at a line so you can inspect every variable in scope, step over or into calls, and watch expressions. Set them by clicking a line number in the Sources panel or by writing a `debugger;` statement in code (remove it before committing). Conditional breakpoints pause only when an expression is true, which is invaluable inside loops.

The Network panel shows every request with its status, headers, and response; the Elements panel shows the live DOM and computed styles. A systematic loop, reproduce, isolate, form a hypothesis, test it, beats scattering `console.log` everywhere.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('47a439f1-5e63-5218-aaff-e5b9eb6bf43c', '5d5d77c3-801f-5824-a943-8c9c2c102a71', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Reading a stack trace$c$, $c$The error was thrown in `formatPrice`, called from `renderRow`, called from `renderTable`. Start at the top.$c$, $c$// TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'toFixed')
//     at formatPrice (cart.js:12:24)
//     at renderRow (cart.js:20:18)
//     at Array.map (<anonymous>)
//     at renderTable (cart.js:27:16)

function formatPrice(item) {
  return item.price.toFixed(2); // line 12: item.price is undefined for one row
}$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('35d527dc-599f-5a4f-b5ba-3650cc702c28', '5d5d77c3-801f-5824-a943-8c9c2c102a71', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Console beyond log$c$, $c$Grouped, tabular, timed output that is easy to scan.$c$, $c$const rows = [
  { sku: 'T1', price: 40 },
  { sku: 'C9', price: undefined },
];

console.group('Cart check');
console.table(rows);
console.time('validate');
rows.forEach((row) => console.assert(typeof row.price === 'number', `bad price for ${row.sku}`));
console.timeEnd('validate');
console.groupEnd();$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('9bb4f336-d0eb-574d-b585-98091f54a0a3', '5d5d77c3-801f-5824-a943-8c9c2c102a71', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Reading the bottom of a stack trace first.
- Logging an object, mutating it, and being confused that the console shows the new value.
- Leaving `debugger` statements or noisy logs in committed code.
- Changing several things at once, so it is unclear which change fixed the bug.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('1aab652f-4167-5cc2-8c67-4a1a5deebe70', '5d5d77c3-801f-5824-a943-8c9c2c102a71', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Debugging rounds hand you broken code and watch your process. Narrate it: "the trace points at line 12; `item.price` is undefined for one row; let me find which row and why." Process matters more than speed.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('64833bcd-de44-5ea1-916d-3000ca484ea0', '5d5d77c3-801f-5824-a943-8c9c2c102a71', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Find the bug: off by one** gives you failing tests and a stack trace to work from.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"find-the-bug-off-by-one"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('853b77ec-15fc-58e6-8f3c-194d26810e41', '1d5e13cf-1519-5864-99da-6bc1c6416a69', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$The DOM is a live object tree$c$, $c$The browser parses HTML into the **Document Object Model**: a tree of element objects that JavaScript can read and change. `document.querySelector(selector)` returns the first element matching a CSS selector (or `null`); `querySelectorAll` returns a static `NodeList` of all matches, which supports `forEach` and can be spread into an array. `getElementById` is a fast special case.

To change what an element shows, set `element.textContent` for plain text. `innerHTML` parses a string as HTML, which is convenient for markup you wrote yourself but dangerous with anything that came from a user or an API, because a string like `<img onerror=…>` executes. Prefer `textContent`, or build elements and append them.

Attributes are read and written with `getAttribute` / `setAttribute` / `removeAttribute`; common ones also exist as properties (`input.value`, `img.src`, `link.href`). `dataset` exposes `data-*` attributes as camel-cased properties. Styling changes belong in classes: `classList.add`, `remove`, `toggle`, and `contains` keep CSS in CSS. Inline `element.style.x` is fine for values computed at runtime, such as a progress width.

A `NodeList` from `querySelectorAll` is a snapshot; elements added later are not in it. The `HTMLCollection` from `getElementsByClassName` is live, which occasionally surprises people iterating while adding.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('e3f940d2-08ba-5a72-93bc-2adb865d2d6f', '1d5e13cf-1519-5864-99da-6bc1c6416a69', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Select, then update text and classes$c$, $c$Text goes through `textContent`; visual state goes through a class.$c$, $c$const badge = document.querySelector('#status');
const rows = document.querySelectorAll('.order-row');

badge.textContent = `${rows.length} orders`;
badge.classList.toggle('is-empty', rows.length === 0);
badge.setAttribute('aria-live', 'polite');

rows.forEach((row, index) => {
  row.dataset.index = String(index);
  row.classList.add(index % 2 ? 'odd' : 'even');
});$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7690979a-1fea-56b8-b6f4-3031f58b9c3c', '1d5e13cf-1519-5864-99da-6bc1c6416a69', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$textContent versus innerHTML$c$, $c$The same untrusted string is harmless as text and dangerous as HTML.$c$, $c$const untrusted = '<img src=x onerror="alert(1)">';
const out = document.querySelector('#out');

out.textContent = untrusted; // shows the literal characters; nothing runs
// out.innerHTML = untrusted; // would create an <img> whose onerror executes

console.log(out.children.length); // 0$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('c0beaf9f-8a70-5c79-80fa-3b454efe25fe', '1d5e13cf-1519-5864-99da-6bc1c6416a69', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Running the script before the elements exist (put it at the end of body or use `defer`).
- Assigning user input to `innerHTML`.
- Forgetting the `#` or `.` in a selector and getting `null`.
- Toggling inline styles when a class would keep the styling in CSS.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('10f59aba-c93b-50a4-a328-4829c7bfd35e', '1d5e13cf-1519-5864-99da-6bc1c6416a69', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"What is the difference between innerHTML and textContent?" is asked constantly and is really a security question. Also expect "how do you select elements?" and a small task like toggling a class on click.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('40a34c3f-6c4e-51ad-bc7c-9789234e4020', '1d5e13cf-1519-5864-99da-6bc1c6416a69', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Render a status badge** is a DOM-free helper that produces the exact class list and text a badge should show.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"render-status-badge"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('eb8eab8e-d0f8-54d2-894e-ff301f31ea29', 'ab2e6a6d-68bf-5339-88bc-21e09e35266b', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Build, insert, remove$c$, $c$`document.createElement('li')` makes a detached element. Set its `textContent`, classes, and attributes, then attach it with `parent.append(child)` (end) or `parent.prepend(child)` (start). Both accept several nodes or strings at once. `element.before(node)` and `element.after(node)` insert siblings, and `element.replaceWith(node)` swaps it out. To remove, call `element.remove()`; the older `parent.removeChild(child)` still works.

Appending an element that is already in the document **moves** it rather than copying it. Use `element.cloneNode(true)` when you want a copy, and remember that clones do not carry event listeners added with `addEventListener`.

Rendering a list means turning data into elements. Building each `<li>` and appending it one by one triggers layout work repeatedly; building them inside a `DocumentFragment` and appending the fragment once is cheaper and is the pattern frameworks optimise internally. When the data changes, the simplest correct approach at this level is to clear the container (`container.replaceChildren()`) and render again.

A `<template>` element in the HTML holds inert markup you can clone with `template.content.cloneNode(true)`, which keeps structure in HTML and logic in JavaScript.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('5cbc1ed8-5b36-5911-8b70-ea00e6ee5c87', 'ab2e6a6d-68bf-5339-88bc-21e09e35266b', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Rendering a list from data$c$, $c$One fragment, one append.$c$, $c$const items = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Tea', done: true },
  { id: 2, name: 'Cake', done: false },
];
const list = document.querySelector('#items');

const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();
for (const item of items) {
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  li.textContent = item.name;
  li.dataset.id = String(item.id);
  li.classList.toggle('done', item.done);
  fragment.append(li);
}
list.replaceChildren(fragment);

console.log(list.children.length); // 2$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('f7564c8b-5815-57d9-b6fc-fb85d070f706', 'ab2e6a6d-68bf-5339-88bc-21e09e35266b', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Moving, cloning, removing$c$, $c$`append` moved the element; `cloneNode` made a second one; `remove` took it out.$c$, $c$const first = list.firstElementChild;
list.append(first);              // moved to the end, still only 2 children
console.log(list.children.length); // 2

const copy = first.cloneNode(true);
list.prepend(copy);
console.log(list.children.length); // 3

copy.remove();
console.log(list.children.length); // 2$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('0403e54e-f2b7-581a-ba86-4b56aa8e645b', 'ab2e6a6d-68bf-5339-88bc-21e09e35266b', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Building HTML strings with user data and assigning to `innerHTML`.
- Expecting `append` to copy an element that is already in the page.
- Adding a listener to each generated item instead of one listener on the list (see event delegation next lesson).
- Appending inside a loop on large lists without a fragment.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d592be9e-b897-558d-bb45-e381bf3728dc', 'ab2e6a6d-68bf-5339-88bc-21e09e35266b', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$A typical task: "given this array, render it as a list, then add a remove button per row." Interviewers look for `createElement` over string concatenation, and for a single delegated listener.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('acb23a08-edbf-51b7-a423-badb7756c4e2', 'ab2e6a6d-68bf-5339-88bc-21e09e35266b', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Build list markup** produces the element structure for a list from data and checks the classes and attributes.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"build-list-markup"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('9fe5b9b3-0e09-5002-a92c-25e0081b9555', '42523dd2-860c-5c14-9f74-02fad6dfb876', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Listen, inspect, delegate$c$, $c$`element.addEventListener(type, handler, options)` registers a function to run when an event of that type reaches the element. You can add several listeners for the same type, and remove one with `removeEventListener` only if you pass the *same function reference*. The `{ once: true }` option removes a listener after its first call.

The handler receives an **event object**. `event.type` is the name; `event.target` is the element where the event *originated*; `event.currentTarget` is the element whose listener is running. `event.preventDefault()` cancels the browser's default action, such as a form submitting or a link navigating. Keyboard events expose `event.key`; mouse events expose coordinates and `button`.

Events **bubble**: after firing on the target, most events fire on each ancestor up to `document`. That enables **delegation**, attaching one listener to a parent and checking `event.target.closest('button')` to see which child was involved. Delegation works for elements added later and avoids thousands of listeners in big lists. `event.stopPropagation()` halts bubbling; use it sparingly because it can break other delegated handlers.

Inside a regular function handler, `this` is `currentTarget`; inside an arrow handler it is whatever the surrounding scope had, so prefer `event.currentTarget` and avoid depending on `this`.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('76c04837-fd61-501b-95c6-5702a425ee00', '42523dd2-860c-5c14-9f74-02fad6dfb876', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Form submit with preventDefault$c$, $c$The page does not reload; the input value is read and cleared.$c$, $c$const form = document.querySelector('#add-item');
const input = form.querySelector('input[name="item"]');
const list = document.querySelector('#items');

form.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  const name = input.value.trim();
  if (!name) return;
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  li.textContent = name;
  const button = document.createElement('button');
  button.textContent = 'Remove';
  button.dataset.action = 'remove';
  li.append(' ', button);
  list.append(li);
  input.value = '';
});$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('12cc2e6b-3b35-5bd4-8ffb-28a101c059de', '42523dd2-860c-5c14-9f74-02fad6dfb876', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$One delegated listener for every row$c$, $c$Rows added later are handled too, because the listener sits on the list.$c$, $c$list.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  const button = event.target.closest('button[data-action="remove"]');
  if (!button || !list.contains(button)) return;
  button.closest('li').remove();
});$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b1d6c471-88d2-5a00-8b00-d331dd50bddd', '42523dd2-860c-5c14-9f74-02fad6dfb876', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- `removeEventListener` with a new arrow function, which never matches.
- Using `event.target` when you meant the element with the listener (`currentTarget`).
- Forgetting `preventDefault` on `submit` and wondering why the page reloads.
- Calling `stopPropagation` by habit and breaking analytics or other delegated handlers.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('1ed34cdb-fa19-5edb-ac68-2b63596c46e9', '42523dd2-860c-5c14-9f74-02fad6dfb876', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"Explain event bubbling and delegation" and "target vs currentTarget" are asked at almost every level. Implementing a delegated click handler on a list is a classic live-coding task.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('346605fe-2f4a-55e3-96f0-2f2ea83f8aee', '42523dd2-860c-5c14-9f74-02fad6dfb876', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Event delegation router** is a pure function that maps a clicked element's attributes to an action, the core of a delegated handler.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"event-delegation-router"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('cdf6198c-ccd7-5eb8-9d9e-2a89047e218a', '0d888304-fd57-55a3-9a37-07967d8d5e00', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Values in, feedback out$c$, $c$Every form control exposes its state as a property. Text-like inputs and `<textarea>` use `.value` (always a string, even for `type="number"`; use `.valueAsNumber` when you need a number). Checkboxes and radios use `.checked`. A `<select>` gives `.value` for the chosen option, and for `multiple` you read `selectedOptions`. Files live in `input.files`.

`new FormData(form)` collects every named control in one object: `formData.get('email')` reads one field, and `Object.fromEntries(formData)` turns it into a plain object for single-valued fields. This is the tidiest way to read a form on submit.

The browser has **constraint validation** built in: attributes such as `required`, `minlength`, `pattern`, and `type="email"` are checked automatically, `form.checkValidity()` reports the result, and `input.setCustomValidity(message)` adds your own rule. Adding `novalidate` to the form lets you run the checks yourself and render errors in your own markup, which is what most apps do for consistent styling.

Listen to `input` for keystroke-by-keystroke changes, `change` when the value is committed (blur, checkbox toggle, select), and `submit` on the form. Always validate on the server too; client-side checks are for user experience, not security.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('717b9dee-0c7b-525c-83d0-9a69b314cba1', '0d888304-fd57-55a3-9a37-07967d8d5e00', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Reading a form with FormData$c$, $c$Number inputs still arrive as strings; convert explicitly.$c$, $c$const form = document.querySelector('#signup');

form.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  const data = Object.fromEntries(new FormData(form));
  const age = Number(data.age);
  const newsletter = form.elements.newsletter.checked;

  console.log(data.email, age, newsletter);
  // 'a@b.c' 29 true
});$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('a1eecc6c-2aac-5f05-9498-0ad218245325', '0d888304-fd57-55a3-9a37-07967d8d5e00', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Custom validation with built-in hooks$c$, $c$The custom message participates in `checkValidity` like any built-in rule.$c$, $c$const password = form.elements.password;
const confirm = form.elements.confirm;

confirm.addEventListener('input', () => {
  confirm.setCustomValidity(confirm.value === password.value ? '' : 'Passwords do not match');
});

form.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
  if (!form.checkValidity()) {
    event.preventDefault();
    form.querySelector(':invalid')?.focus();
  }
});$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('60ee2ffe-4f89-52b1-bb61-9ebc0a94c538', '0d888304-fd57-55a3-9a37-07967d8d5e00', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Treating `input.value` as a number.
- Reading `.value` of a checkbox instead of `.checked`.
- Validating only on the client.
- Listening to `change` on a text field and expecting per-keystroke updates.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('62e273b9-4b40-5dba-b3ae-1e8a69a5e5fb', '0d888304-fd57-55a3-9a37-07967d8d5e00', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Frontend interviews love a small form task: validate, show errors, prevent the reload. Mentioning `FormData`, constraint validation, and server-side validation shows breadth.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('9a8a2fd1-83f2-5261-9071-eee17124f4b8', '0d888304-fd57-55a3-9a37-07967d8d5e00', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Validate a sign-up form** implements the rules as a pure function returning an errors object.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"validate-signup-form"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8abcdcef-3f2d-5d99-ac70-d8e4cbf6bfb3', '0d888304-fd57-55a3-9a37-07967d8d5e00', 7, $c$summary$c$, $c$Key takeaways$c$, $c$Read text-like controls with .value (always a string), checkboxes and radios with .checked, and whole forms with FormData. Listen to input for live changes, change for committed values, and submit on the form itself, calling preventDefault to stay on the page. Let the browser's constraint validation do the routine checks, add custom rules with setCustomValidity, and always validate again on the server because client checks are for experience, not security.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b7951313-ba8b-592d-a659-c11cbfbe0a8b', 'd869030a-e6e2-5324-b607-33c07e99387a', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$One thread, several queues$c$, $c$JavaScript runs on a single thread with a **call stack**. When you call `setTimeout(fn, 500)`, the browser starts a timer and returns immediately; `fn` is placed on the **task queue** once the delay has passed. The **event loop** takes the next task only when the call stack is empty, so a delay is a minimum, not a guarantee. `setTimeout(fn, 0)` still waits for the current work to finish. `setInterval` repeats until `clearInterval`; both return an id you pass to `clearTimeout` / `clearInterval` to cancel.

Promise reactions (`.then`, `await` continuations) go on the **microtask queue**, which is drained completely after each task and before the browser renders. That is why a resolved promise's callback runs before a `setTimeout(…, 0)` callback, and why a long chain of microtasks can starve rendering.

The order for a typical snippet is therefore: all synchronous code, then every queued microtask, then the next task (a timer or an event), then microtasks again, and so on. Long synchronous loops block everything, including clicks and animations, which is what "do not block the main thread" means.

A common practical use is **debouncing**: reset a timer on every input event and run the expensive work only after the user pauses.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('2f763e78-f8fe-535e-a073-ec12115d12ff', 'd869030a-e6e2-5324-b607-33c07e99387a', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Predicting order$c$, $c$Sync first, microtask second, timer last, even with a zero delay.$c$, $c$console.log('start');

setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout'), 0);

Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('microtask'));

console.log('end');

// start
// end
// microtask
// timeout$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('0cc94bcd-af90-599b-a196-1c28e5efff57', 'd869030a-e6e2-5324-b607-33c07e99387a', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$A countdown with clearInterval$c$, $c$The id returned by `setInterval` is what stops it.$c$, $c$function countdown(seconds, onTick, onDone) {
  let remaining = seconds;
  const id = setInterval(() => {
    remaining -= 1;
    onTick(remaining);
    if (remaining === 0) {
      clearInterval(id);
      onDone();
    }
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id); // cancel function
}

const cancel = countdown(3, (n) => console.log(n), () => console.log('done'));
// cancel(); would stop it early$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('79a71054-9bfc-5119-bbae-17aa3d351f30', 'd869030a-e6e2-5324-b607-33c07e99387a', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Believing `setTimeout(fn, 0)` runs immediately.
- Forgetting to clear intervals when a component or page section goes away, leaking work.
- Passing `fn()` (the call) instead of `fn` to `setTimeout`.
- Running heavy synchronous loops and wondering why the UI freezes.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('da2a0c9b-544d-58a4-9731-f90cdfd55b31', 'd869030a-e6e2-5324-b607-33c07e99387a', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$Output-order questions mixing `setTimeout`, promises, and `console.log` appear in most JavaScript interviews. Draw the stack, microtask queue, and task queue; walk through the snippet in that order.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('2216dc27-92cc-57a9-85b9-99b281d52a61', 'd869030a-e6e2-5324-b607-33c07e99387a', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Timed sequence** schedules callbacks with setTimeout, reports the order they fire, and supports cancelling the rest.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"timed-sequence"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('fd751284-347e-5e94-b70f-e5f3b36d5c75', 'cfc60154-df02-54ea-8407-ca737371ef01', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Request, response, body$c$, $c$`fetch(url, options)` starts an HTTP request and returns a promise for a **Response**. The promise resolves as soon as headers arrive, and it resolves even for `404` or `500`; it rejects only when the request could not be made at all (network down, blocked by CORS, aborted). So the first thing to do with a response is check `response.ok` (status 200–299) or `response.status`.

The body is read with another promise-returning method: `response.json()`, `response.text()`, or `response.blob()`. The body can be read only once. `json()` throws if the body is not valid JSON, which is why an error page returned with a 500 can produce a `SyntaxError` if you skipped the `ok` check.

`async` functions let you write this as straight-line code: `await` pauses the function until the promise settles and returns its value, or throws its rejection so `try/catch` works. An `async` function always returns a promise, so callers `await` it too. Run independent requests together with `Promise.all([...])` rather than awaiting them one after another.

To send data, pass `method: 'POST'`, a `headers` object with `'Content-Type': 'application/json'`, and `body: JSON.stringify(payload)`. An `AbortController` lets you cancel a request, for example when the user types a new search before the previous one returns.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('df78054c-f025-5be0-b131-b4bb4d3de9e6', 'cfc60154-df02-54ea-8407-ca737371ef01', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$A reusable JSON helper$c$, $c$Checks `ok`, parses JSON, and turns HTTP errors into thrown errors with status.$c$, $c$async function getJSON(url, options = {}) {
  const response = await fetch(url, options);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`, { cause: response });
  }
  return response.json();
}

async function loadMenu() {
  try {
    const [items, offers] = await Promise.all([
      getJSON('/api/items'),
      getJSON('/api/offers'),
    ]);
    console.log(items.length, offers.length);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Could not load menu', error.message);
  }
}$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('14513e63-971f-533f-b12a-0f997d4d4a3f', 'cfc60154-df02-54ea-8407-ca737371ef01', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Sending JSON$c$, $c$Method, header, and a stringified body.$c$, $c$async function createOrder(order) {
  return getJSON('/api/orders', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify(order),
  });
}$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('629ac013-95f7-51c4-b569-5ee574babe79', 'cfc60154-df02-54ea-8407-ca737371ef01', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Assuming `fetch` rejects on a 404.
- Forgetting `await` in front of `response.json()` and getting a promise instead of data.
- Awaiting independent requests sequentially.
- Reading the body twice.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('016bb420-b24c-59b9-8d92-04b94df8d123', 'cfc60154-df02-54ea-8407-ca737371ef01', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"What does fetch reject on?" and "how would you fetch two resources in parallel?" are frequent. Writing the `getJSON` helper from memory, with the `ok` check, is a strong signal.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('ffb684a5-1d72-5361-937b-3001bc99c5f1', 'cfc60154-df02-54ea-8407-ca737371ef01', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**fetch JSON helper** asks for exactly this wrapper, tested with a fake fetch.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"fetch-json-helper"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('24538369-e6d1-588e-9c6f-bb7cad74fc08', '5d53e626-e89e-5c07-b28c-2dc442f4a334', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Files as modules$c$, $c$An ES module is a file whose top-level bindings are private unless exported. **Named exports** (`export function add …`, `export const rate = …`) can be many per file and are imported by name in braces: `import { add, rate } from './math.js'`. A **default export** (`export default …`) is one per file and is imported with any local name: `import calc from './math.js'`. Rename with `as`, and gather everything with `import * as math from './math.js'`.

Modules are always in strict mode, have their own scope (no accidental globals), and are **evaluated once**: every importer shares the same instance, so a module-level variable acts as shared state. Imports are live bindings to the exporter's variables, not copies. Import and export statements are hoisted and resolved before the module body runs, so circular imports work but can expose values that are not initialised yet.

In the browser, load a module with `<script type="module" src="app.js">`. Module scripts are deferred automatically, so the DOM is ready when they run, and they are fetched with CORS rules, which is why opening a page from `file://` often fails and a local server is needed. Specifiers must be paths (`./`, `../`, `/`) or full URLs unless an import map defines bare names. `import()` as a function loads a module on demand and returns a promise, which is how bundlers split code.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('adefe42d-9d7e-568d-9b43-145ed8546470', '5d53e626-e89e-5c07-b28c-2dc442f4a334', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Named, default, and namespace imports$c$, $c$One module, three ways to import from it.$c$, $c$// math.js
export const TAX_RATE = 0.18;
export function withTax(amount) {
  return amount * (1 + TAX_RATE);
}
export default function formatINR(amount) {
  return `₹${amount.toFixed(2)}`;
}

// app.js
import formatINR, { withTax, TAX_RATE as rate } from './math.js';
import * as math from './math.js';

console.log(formatINR(withTax(100))); // '₹118.00'
console.log(rate === math.TAX_RATE);   // true$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d03a05e6-6638-5854-9fc2-ae8587e81af5', '5d53e626-e89e-5c07-b28c-2dc442f4a334', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$Single evaluation and shared state$c$, $c$Both importers see the same counter because the module ran once.$c$, $c$// counter.js
let count = 0;
export function increment() { return ++count; }

// a.js
import { increment } from './counter.js';
console.log(increment()); // 1

// b.js
import { increment } from './counter.js';
console.log(increment()); // 2 (same module instance)$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('624eaff1-dc0b-5cb4-aabf-8c8e0c7c7871', '5d53e626-e89e-5c07-b28c-2dc442f4a334', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- `import { default } …` or braces around a default import.
- Omitting the file extension or the `./` prefix in a browser specifier.
- Expecting a top-level variable in a module to be a global.
- Mixing `require` (CommonJS) with `import` in the same browser file.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('88bb90b0-ffb0-5e99-973d-1260a39a88fe', '5d53e626-e89e-5c07-b28c-2dc442f4a334', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"Named vs default export?", "what does type=module change?", and "are imports copies?" are common. Knowing that modules are evaluated once and imports are live bindings answers several follow-ups at once.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d9925867-8acf-5137-a7e5-5d7f0a93a7f4', '5d53e626-e89e-5c07-b28c-2dc442f4a334', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Module registry** simulates a module's single-evaluation behaviour with a factory and a cache.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"module-registry"}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('bb7ff5a5-cce3-5bd7-a43a-cc8f649bfb1e', '142d043d-0650-5992-9683-7aab3b47559b', 1, $c$explanation$c$, $c$Persistence and a shape for small apps$c$, $c$`localStorage` is a per-origin key/value store that survives page reloads and browser restarts. It stores **strings only**, so objects go through `JSON.stringify` on the way in and `JSON.parse` on the way out. `setItem`, `getItem` (returns `null` when missing), `removeItem`, and `clear` are synchronous. `sessionStorage` has the same API but lasts only for the tab.

Because it is synchronous, string-only, limited to a few megabytes, and readable by any script on the origin, localStorage is for small preferences and drafts, never for secrets or large data; `IndexedDB` handles big structured data and a server handles anything shared. Reads can throw in private modes or when storage is disabled, so wrap them in `try/catch` and fall back to defaults.

A small app stays understandable when it has one **state** object, one **render** function that draws the whole UI from state, and event handlers that only change state and then call render. Saving is then one line inside a `setState` helper. This unidirectional shape is what every frontend framework formalises, so building it once by hand makes React and Angular much easier to learn.

The `storage` event fires in *other* tabs when a value changes, which is a simple way to keep two open tabs in sync.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('0049fd72-a09a-5f55-946a-db123b138f44', '142d043d-0650-5992-9683-7aab3b47559b', 2, $c$example$c$, $c$Load and save with safe defaults$c$, $c$Missing or corrupt data falls back to an empty list instead of crashing.$c$, $c$const KEY = 'todo:v1';

function load() {
  try {
    const raw = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
    const parsed = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : [];
    return Array.isArray(parsed) ? parsed : [];
  } catch {
    return [];
  }
}

function save(todos) {
  try {
    localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(todos));
  } catch {
    // storage full or disabled: keep working in memory
  }
}$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('4252a60e-476e-5841-88d3-ce6911fff7dd', '142d043d-0650-5992-9683-7aab3b47559b', 3, $c$example$c$, $c$State, render, events$c$, $c$Handlers change state and re-render; nothing else touches the DOM.$c$, $c$let todos = load();
const list = document.querySelector('#todos');
const form = document.querySelector('#new-todo');

function setState(next) {
  todos = next;
  save(todos);
  render();
}

function render() {
  list.replaceChildren(...todos.map((todo) => {
    const li = document.createElement('li');
    li.textContent = todo.text;
    li.dataset.id = todo.id;
    li.classList.toggle('done', todo.done);
    return li;
  }));
}

form.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  const text = form.elements.text.value.trim();
  if (text) setState([...todos, { id: crypto.randomUUID(), text, done: false }]);
  form.reset();
});

list.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  const id = event.target.closest('li')?.dataset.id;
  if (id) setState(todos.map((t) => (t.id === id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t)));
});

render();$c$, $c$javascript$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('a518e207-de1d-54fc-b18c-21cf11430311', '142d043d-0650-5992-9683-7aab3b47559b', 4, $c$common_mistakes$c$, $c$Common mistakes$c$, $c$- Storing objects without stringify (you get `'[object Object]'`).
- Storing tokens or personal data in localStorage.
- Updating the DOM directly in handlers so the saved state and the screen drift apart.
- Not versioning the key, so an old shape breaks a new release.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('557b8f8a-6b10-50db-b15d-ba55a4f8383e', '142d043d-0650-5992-9683-7aab3b47559b', 5, $c$interview_relevance$c$, $c$How this shows up in interviews$c$, $c$"localStorage vs sessionStorage vs cookies" is a standard question; so is a take-home to-do app. Interviewers look for the state → render → events structure and for graceful handling of missing or corrupt storage.$c$, null, null, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('2515207e-87d8-5f0f-b26c-2b62a8fe673f', '142d043d-0650-5992-9683-7aab3b47559b', 6, $c$practice_link$c$, $c$Practice$c$, $c$**Todo store** implements the state operations and a storage adapter that the UI above would call.$c$, null, null, $c${"problem":"todo-store"}$c$::jsonb, 1)
