('297d98dc-8c4d-51ec-91ca-941f4ea7932a', $c$js-timers-001$c$, $c$output$c$, $c$Write the output, one value per line.$c$, $c${"code":"console.log(1);\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(2), 0);\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));\nqueueMicrotask(() => console.log(4));\nconsole.log(5);"}$c$::jsonb, $c$Sync first, then microtasks in order, then the timer task.$c$, 2, 1, 'published', array[$c$event-loop$c$, $c$microtasks$c$]::text[]),
('44af1504-cdbb-5392-85ea-e9066f9c725f', $c$js-timers-002$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$Roughly when does 'done' print?$c$, $c${"code":"setTimeout(() => console.log('done'), 100);\nconst start = Date.now();\nwhile (Date.now() - start < 500) {}\nconsole.log('loop over');","options":["After 'loop over', about 500 ms after start","About 100 ms after start, before 'loop over'","Immediately","Never, because the loop blocks the timer permanently"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Timer callbacks wait for the call stack to empty.$c$, 2, 1, 'published', array[$c$setTimeout$c$, $c$blocking$c$]::text[]),
('1d2cccf6-3e9b-537d-8a4e-72f98d155132', $c$js-timers-003$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$What is wrong with this code?$c$, $c${"code":"let ticks = 0;\nsetInterval(() => {\n  ticks++;\n  if (ticks === 3) clearInterval();\n}, 1000);","options":["clearInterval needs the id returned by setInterval, so the interval never stops","setInterval cannot be cleared from inside its own callback","ticks must be declared with var","The interval stops automatically after three runs"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Timers are cancelled by id.$c$, 1, 1, 'published', array[$c$setInterval$c$, $c$clearInterval$c$]::text[]),
('397ae61d-3e3f-5894-bf33-e9222ef7ced7', $c$js-fetch-001$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$The server responds with 404. What happens?$c$, $c${"code":"try {\n  const res = await fetch('/api/missing');\n  console.log(res.ok, res.status);\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log('network error');\n}","options":["Prints false 404","Prints 'network error'","Throws an uncaught error","Prints true 404"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$fetch resolves for any HTTP response; it rejects only when no response could be obtained.$c$, 2, 1, 'published', array[$c$fetch$c$, $c$response.ok$c$]::text[]),
('59878d7b-8fd2-517d-a2a9-bbd34acdeb60', $c$js-fetch-002$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$Each request takes about 1 second. How long does loadAll take?$c$, $c${"code":"async function loadAll() {\n  const a = await fetch('/a');\n  const b = await fetch('/b');\n  return [a, b];\n}","options":["About 2 seconds, because the awaits run one after another","About 1 second, because fetch calls start immediately","About 0 seconds, because fetch is non-blocking","It depends on the order of resolution"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Sequential awaits serialise independent requests.$c$, 2, 1, 'published', array[$c$async-await$c$, $c$Promise.all$c$]::text[]),
('63605f64-b052-5500-904a-e3b236941506', $c$js-fetch-003$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$What does this print?$c$, $c${"code":"async function load() {\n  const res = await fetch('/api/user');\n  const user = res.json();\n  console.log(typeof user.name);\n}","options":["'undefined', because user is a pending promise","'string'","'object'","It throws because json() needs an argument"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$response.json() returns a promise; it must be awaited.$c$, 1, 1, 'published', array[$c$response.json$c$, $c$await$c$]::text[]),
('3766a113-daed-5681-b67b-d326d1adc3be', $c$js-modules-001$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$Which import statement is invalid for this module?$c$, $c${"code":"// util.js\nexport const a = 1;\nexport default function main() {}","options":["import { default } from './util.js';","import main from './util.js';","import main, { a } from './util.js';","import * as util from './util.js';"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$default is a reserved word in that position; use import name from or { default as name }.$c$, 2, 1, 'published', array[$c$modules$c$, $c$default-export$c$]::text[]),
('702bd1c5-394c-5351-828d-3c140efaf2ce', $c$js-modules-002$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$What does this print?$c$, $c${"code":"// counter.js\nexport let count = 0;\nexport function bump() { count++; }\n\n// app.js\nimport { count, bump } from './counter.js';\nbump();\nbump();\nconsole.log(count);","options":["2","0","undefined","It throws because count is read-only"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Imports are live bindings to the exporter's variables.$c$, 2, 1, 'published', array[$c$modules$c$, $c$live-bindings$c$]::text[]),
('8d91bb90-25d4-5f3b-a30b-a6cd27ae16ae', $c$js-modules-003$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$What is true about <script type="module" src="app.js">?$c$, $c${"code":"","options":["It is deferred by default and runs in strict mode with its own scope","It blocks HTML parsing until it has run","Its top-level variables become globals","It can use require() to load other files"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Module scripts are deferred, strict, and scoped.$c$, 1, 1, 'published', array[$c$type-module$c$, $c$defer$c$]::text[]),
('d7f5f33a-ca5e-54d9-8ec3-8cc404cab0ec', $c$js-storage-001$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$What does this print?$c$, $c${"code":"localStorage.setItem('user', { name: 'Ann' });\nconsole.log(localStorage.getItem('user'));","options":["'[object Object]'","'{\"name\":\"Ann\"}'","{ name: 'Ann' }","null"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$localStorage stores strings; objects are converted with toString.$c$, 1, 1, 'published', array[$c$localStorage$c$, $c$JSON$c$]::text[]),
('b23895a0-9f3f-5f90-b6b7-8c3bf0d3ff37', $c$js-storage-002$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$Which statement about web storage is correct?$c$, $c${"code":"","options":["localStorage persists across browser restarts; sessionStorage is cleared when the tab closes","sessionStorage is shared across all tabs of the origin","localStorage is sent to the server with every request","Both can store objects directly"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The two stores differ only in lifetime and tab scope.$c$, 1, 1, 'published', array[$c$localStorage$c$, $c$sessionStorage$c$]::text[]),
('8c231308-f611-518c-840f-7fad65fd0b1b', $c$js-storage-003$c$, $c$single_choice$c$, $c$In a state → render → events app, where should the DOM be updated when a todo is toggled?$c$, $c${"code":"","options":["Inside render, after the handler updates state and calls it","Inside the click handler, directly on the clicked element","Inside the save function, right before writing to storage","Inside load, when the app starts"]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Handlers change state; render draws it.$c$, 2, 1, 'published', array[$c$app-structure$c$, $c$state$c$]::text[])
on conflict (slug) do update set question_type = excluded.question_type, prompt = excluded.prompt, payload = excluded.payload, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, version = excluded.version, status = excluded.status, topic_tags = excluded.topic_tags, updated_at = now();
insert into public.question_answer_keys (question_id, answer, rationale, misconceptions, version) values
('10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Multiplication has higher precedence than addition, so 3 * 4 is evaluated before adding 2.$c$, '{}'::jsonb, 2),
('10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Objects are assigned by sharing a reference to the same object. Changing copy.name changes the object that user also points to.$c$, '{}'::jsonb, 2),
('10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000008', $c${"correct":[2]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Number converts an empty string to 0. The string 'false' is non-empty, so Boolean converts it to true.$c$, '{}'::jsonb, 2),
('10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010', $c${"correct":[2]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Loose equality converts false to 0, making the first comparison true. Strict equality sees different types, so the second is false.$c$, '{}'::jsonb, 2),
('10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The returned function closes over one count binding. The first call increments it to 1 and the second increments the same binding to 2.$c$, '{}'::jsonb, 2),
('10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', $c${"correct":[2]}$c$::jsonb, $c$A and C run on the current call stack. The promise handler runs afterward as a microtask, producing A, C, B.$c$, '{}'::jsonb, 2),
('d6d8f627-db20-54fd-a7bf-8dde636bfa74', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$const prevents reassigning the binding, not mutating the value it points to. push mutates the array in place and succeeds; assigning a new array to list throws a TypeError.$c$, $c${"0":"Reassigning a const binding is an error at runtime.","2":"const does not freeze the array; Object.freeze would be needed for that.","3":"Reassignment of a const is a runtime TypeError, not a parse-time SyntaxError."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('5d90a928-5294-5b0a-9437-335ed21b65e8', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$typeof null is 'object' (a long-standing quirk), arrays are objects so typeof [] is 'object', and functions report 'function'. Use Array.isArray to detect arrays.$c$, $c${"0":"There is no 'null' or 'array' result from typeof.","2":"Functions are the one object type that typeof reports specially as 'function'.","3":"typeof null is 'object', not 'null'."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('857f5609-874a-5450-81b7-cc362213b12a', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$n++ evaluates to the old value (5) and then increments n to 6; ++n increments first to 7 and evaluates to 7. n ends at 7.$c$, $c${"1":"Postfix ++ returns the value before incrementing.","2":"Two increments happened, so n is 7, not 6.","3":"Prefix ++ returns the value after incrementing, so b is 7."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('2c48b3d4-2fec-5448-8054-221fc0330584', $c${"correct":[2]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The conditional (ternary) operator is an expression that evaluates to a value, so it can be assigned or returned. if, for, and let declarations are statements.$c$, $c${"0":"An if statement does not produce a value.","1":"A for loop is a statement.","3":"A declaration is a statement, even though it contains the expression 0."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('274a1865-11e5-58ad-b210-a1d45f74ae2f', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$1 + '2' concatenates to '12'. '3' - 1 converts to numbers giving 2. true + 1 is 2. [] converts to '' and {} to '[object Object]', so the result is '[object Object]'.$c$, $c${"1":"With a string operand, + concatenates rather than adds.","2":"- and boolean + number convert to numbers; only + with a string concatenates.","3":"[] + {} concatenates their string forms rather than producing NaN."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('9611a1d8-713f-5038-85cd-19bd8bf2c811', $c${"correct":[2]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Truthy: '0' (non-empty string), ' ' (non-empty string), [] (any object), 'false' (non-empty string). Falsy: 0, '', null, NaN. That is four truthy values.$c$, $c${"0":"Non-empty strings such as '0' and 'false' are truthy.","1":"An empty array is an object and therefore truthy.","3":"0, '', null, and NaN are all falsy."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('136c535e-9e08-50de-b357-55688fd78fb4', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$null and undefined are loosely equal only to each other, so null == undefined is true and null == 0 is false. Strict equality of different types is false. NaN is never equal to anything, including itself.$c$, $c${"1":"null and undefined are different types, so === is false.","2":"null does not coerce to 0 for ==; it only equals undefined.","3":"null == undefined is the one case where they are equal."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('890c07a5-def2-5e9a-82a2-cc4614af583a', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$0 is falsy, so count || 10 gives 10. ?? only replaces null or undefined, so count ?? 10 keeps 0. && returns the first falsy operand, which is 0.$c$, $c${"1":"|| replaces falsy values such as 0 with the right-hand side.","2":"?? does not treat 0 as missing.","3":"&& short-circuits on the falsy 0 and returns it."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('3df0a924-0327-57f1-9ee1-fb0a72e419bc', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$With var there is a single i shared by every iteration. By the time the timers run, the loop has finished and i is 3, so each callback logs 3. Using let would create a fresh i per iteration and print 0 1 2.$c$, $c${"0":"That is the result with let, which creates a per-iteration binding.","2":"The loop has already advanced i to 3 when the callbacks run.","3":"i is defined; it is simply the final value 3."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('87075b66-4006-5ffc-859e-7dfd8089b4d3', $c${"correct":[2]}$c$::jsonb, $c$let and const are hoisted into the block but sit in the temporal dead zone until their declaration runs. Reading label before that line throws a ReferenceError. A var would print undefined instead.$c$, $c${"0":"That is the behaviour of var, whose binding is initialised to undefined.","1":"The declaration has not executed yet when the log runs.","3":"Nothing sets label to null."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d8728ff7-1603-5d90-bf1c-b4ab0d286397', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Returning a plain value from an async function wraps it in a resolved promise. Logging the call shows a Promise object; await get() or get().then would give 5.$c$, $c${"0":"The value is wrapped in a promise; you must await it.","2":"The function explicitly returns 5, wrapped in a promise.","3":"Calling an async function without await is allowed."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7da8fe63-22ae-5041-9b99-84c4425d2b40', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$A rejection anywhere earlier in the chain, including an exception thrown inside the then callback, skips remaining then handlers and lands in the next catch.$c$, $c${"0":"Errors thrown inside then callbacks also reach the catch.","2":"catch runs only when a rejection reaches it.","3":"Chained handlers run in sequence, each waiting for the previous promise."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('92c4476a-034b-5c44-abdf-0a1d1cf9732b', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$case 2 matches and logs 'two'. There is no break, so execution falls into case 3 and logs 'three', then break stops it before default.$c$, $c${"0":"Without break, execution continues into the next case.","2":"The break in case 3 prevents reaching default.","3":"Matching starts at case 2, which logs first."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('c30c2502-1585-5e0a-a0eb-3580806109f3', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$role = 'admin' assigns and evaluates to 'admin', which is truthy, so the if branch runs and prints 'access'. The intended comparison was role === 'admin'.$c$, $c${"0":"The assignment makes the condition truthy, so the else branch never runs.","2":"Assignment inside a condition is valid syntax, which is exactly why it is dangerous.","3":"The if branch executes and logs."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('e8cb6607-4c8d-575f-ad1e-fd91adc6f000', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$switch uses ===, and the string '2' is not strictly equal to the number 2, so no case matches and default runs.$c$, $c${"0":"That would require loose equality, which switch does not use.","2":"Comparing different types is not an error.","3":"default runs only because no case matched."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('761e2956-7fd2-5f91-8e07-703e571f582c', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$for...in iterates enumerable string keys, so it logs the indexes '0' and '1' (printed as 0 and 1). for...of iterates values, logging 'a' and 'b'.$c$, $c${"0":"for...in does not yield values.","2":"for...of yields the values 'a' and 'b', not indexes.","3":"The order is keys first (for...in), then values (for...of)."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('e9e857b7-9a73-512c-a58f-c6a7f029135a', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The condition i <= nums.length runs the body for i = 3 as well, and nums[3] is undefined, which is concatenated as the text 'undefined'.$c$, $c${"0":"The loop runs one extra time because of <=.","2":"Out-of-range array reads return undefined rather than throwing.","3":"All three items and the extra undefined are appended."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('885a797f-c0ed-575a-b87b-7116af23a190', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$1 is added (sum 1), 2 skipped, 3 added (sum 4), 4 skipped, 5 triggers break. The result is 4.$c$, $c${"1":"5 is never added because break runs before the addition.","2":"Even numbers are skipped by continue.","3":"Neither even numbers nor 5 contribute."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('f200047d-5165-5966-a11b-d682dd034580', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$0 is a valid number, but !0 is true, so the guard returns null instead of 0. The guard should check n == null or typeof n !== 'number'.$c$, $c${"0":"5 is truthy and doubles correctly.","2":"-3 is truthy and doubles to -6.","3":"2.5 is truthy and doubles to 5."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('f6e67817-37e7-5444-91cb-e44a29b75604', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The second guard matches and returns 'Failed'. The final console.log is unreachable in every path.$c$, $c${"1":"The log after return is unreachable.","2":"The status matched an earlier guard.","3":"Nothing executes after the function returns."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8b55648f-afd6-5c3f-b14b-e9167414aae3', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$'warn' maps to '!'. 'error' is missing, so ?? gives '?'. 'toString' is inherited from Object.prototype and is a function, so it is returned (printed as its source). Use Object.hasOwn or a Map to avoid inherited keys.$c$, $c${"0":"toString exists on every plain object via the prototype chain.","2":"?? replaces undefined with '?'.","3":"The inherited value is the function itself, not that string."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8b9f5788-28a5-5f93-a882-0201541da3f4', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$a is a function declaration, so calling it early works and prints 'A'. b is a const holding an arrow function and is in the temporal dead zone at the time of the call, so a ReferenceError is thrown.$c$, $c${"0":"b cannot be called before its declaration line.","2":"The declaration a is fully hoisted and prints first.","3":"Function declarations are hoisted with their bodies, not as undefined."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('1697700e-a29d-5b5d-9e3d-4f00812e6b1c', $c${"correct":[1]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The body is a block containing a labelled statement id: 1 and no return, so the function returns undefined. Wrap the object in parentheses: () => ({ id: 1 }).$c$, $c${"0":"Returning an object literal requires parentheses around the braces.","2":"The label id: 1 is a statement; nothing is returned.","3":"A labelled statement is valid syntax, which is why this bug is silent."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('27c5011e-d619-51e8-bf45-658fccafb789…260 tokens truncated…,"3":"The function returns others.length, not others."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('e9bded07-08ef-53cb-9c62-a48125e63f7c', $c${"correct":[2]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The top-level name property is copied, so changing b.name does not affect a. The tags array is shared by reference, so pushing through b changes a.tags too.$c$, $c${"0":"Nested objects are shared, so the push is visible through a.","1":"Top-level primitives are copied; a.name stays 'A'.","3":"The nested array is shared, not copied."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('35cc887b-b87e-5e2b-8223-4d4ab57c71b2', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$tick() is evaluated right away, printing 'tick' and returning undefined, which is what setTimeout receives. Pass tick (the function) instead.$c$, $c${"1":"The call happens synchronously before setTimeout is even invoked.","2":"Passing undefined as a callback does not throw in most environments; it just does nothing.","3":"It runs once, immediately."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('733d6c6a-171e-5d2b-b43a-c246c4827461', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$add10 remembers x = 10 and add20 remembers x = 20; they are independent closures. makeAdder(5)(5) creates and immediately calls a third one: 10.$c$, $c${"1":"The second closure has its own x = 20.","2":"Each call to makeAdder creates a separate scope.","3":"makeAdder(5)(5) adds 5 + 5."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('dbc930d0-4f9d-5d4a-a670-758b35d223f6', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Array iteration methods run their callbacks immediately, before returning, so the numbers are logged between 'a' and 'b'.$c$, $c${"1":"forEach is not asynchronous; it does not defer the callbacks.","2":"'a' is logged before forEach is called.","3":"The callbacks do run and log 1 and 2."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('87f695f5-888c-55a4-9ee8-e49e7474909b', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$player.key reads the property literally named key (1). player[key] evaluates key to 'score' and reads that property (9).$c$, $c${"1":"Dot notation does not look up the variable key.","2":"Bracket notation evaluates the variable, giving 'score'.","3":"The object does have a property literally named key."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8ad8fe40-3da7-56a6-a5a3-16c480cd9eb2', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$debug exists (with value undefined), so 'debug' in config is true and its value is undefined. level does not exist, so in is false, but reading it still yields undefined.$c$, $c${"1":"The key debug is present even though its value is undefined.","2":"level was never added, so in is false.","3":"config.debug is undefined, so the comparison is true."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b8a69a76-2e4d-5266-b232-4ff8add34b27', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Object keys are always strings, so Object.keys returns ['1', '2']. a and b are different objects, so === is false. Their JSON text is identical, so the string comparison is true.$c$, $c${"1":"Numeric-looking keys are converted to strings.","2":"Two separate object literals are never === to each other.","3":"Keys are strings and the JSON strings match."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d95e312b-cde6-5ed5-84af-3b1ec19790ef', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$user.hello() is called on user, so this is user and returns 'Ira'. fn() is called without an object, so this is undefined in strict mode; this?.name evaluates to undefined without throwing.$c$, $c${"1":"The detached call has no receiver, so this is not user.","2":"The first call is made on user and does have this.","3":"Optional chaining prevents the TypeError that this.name would throw."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('97a0c185-d47e-50a5-8100-2476e09bfbae', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The arrow function has no this of its own, so it uses the this of start, which was called on timer. seconds becomes 1. A regular function callback would have lost this.$c$, $c${"1":"this.seconds is 0, not undefined, because this is timer.","2":"The arrow keeps the method's this, so seconds is a number.","3":"No error occurs; this is defined via the arrow's lexical scope."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('ed0770a8-8597-575e-b928-fcbeeb057aad', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$who.call(b) sets this to b. boundA is permanently bound to a, so it returns 'a' even when invoked with call(b).$c$, $c${"1":"call cannot re-bind a function created by bind.","2":"who.call(b) explicitly sets this to b.","3":"boundA is bound to a, not b."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7ff28660-830b-5670-977f-e84acc22d33e', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$a is present but undefined, so the default 'x' applies and is stored in first. b is missing, so its default 'y' applies. rest collects the remaining own keys c and d.$c$, $c${"1":"A property whose value is undefined still triggers the default.","2":"b was consumed by the pattern (with a default) and is not part of rest.","3":"Missing keys get their defaults."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('87287f08-a2d3-5672-b4e8-23dab6501060', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$items.length is 0, and ?? only replaces null/undefined, so 0 stays. meta is missing, so res.meta?.page is undefined and ?? gives 1. items[0] is undefined, so ?.id yields undefined without throwing.$c$, $c${"1":"0 is not nullish, so ?? keeps it.","2":"?? replaces the undefined page with 1.","3":"Optional chaining prevents the TypeError."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('3cc437d0-e403-5ab1-be07-2c7572a5f0d6', $c${"correct":[2]}$c$::jsonb, $c$With no argument, the parameter is undefined and the pattern tries to read host from undefined, which throws a TypeError. connect({}) works because defaults fill in missing keys. Writing ({ … } = {}) fixes it.$c$, $c${"0":"An empty object destructures fine; defaults apply.","1":"port is provided and host defaults.","3":"host is provided and port defaults."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b1d0f8d1-2fe8-52f1-afa1-3e7183f62bc0', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$push appends both items and returns the array's new length, 3. It does not return the array or the last item.$c$, $c${"1":"push returns a number, not the array; that is why chaining push does not work.","2":"push does not return the pushed item.","3":"Two items were pushed to an array of one, giving length 3."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('0377b1d1-87df-55e3-a6a3-14883962a4f3', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$slice(1, 3) copies indexes 1 and 2 without changing a. splice(1, 2) removes two items starting at index 1, returning [2, 3] and leaving a as [1, 4].$c$, $c${"1":"splice mutates the original array.","2":"slice's end index is exclusive.","3":"splice returns the removed items, not the remaining ones."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b3604c09-0cba-5979-b253-c7f4f922895e', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$sort mutates in place. alias and original are the same array, so original is now sorted. copy was created before the sort and is unaffected.$c$, $c${"1":"sort mutates the array that both variables reference.","2":"The spread copy is a different array and was not sorted.","3":"It is the original (via alias) that was sorted, not the copy."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('365080ec-4da0-5168-81ef-a4cd83963e23', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$With an initial value, reduce on an empty array simply returns it (0). Without one, there is nothing to use as the accumulator, so reduce throws a TypeError.$c$, $c${"1":"The second call throws rather than returning undefined.","2":"Without an initial value there is no accumulator to return.","3":"The first call returns the initial value 0."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d0ceef2f-2e73-5ab7-a936-251ca16857f9', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$filter keeps 1 and 3 because their remainders are truthy. map then multiplies each by 10.$c$, $c${"1":"Even numbers give a remainder of 0, which is falsy, so they are removed.","2":"filter removed the even numbers.","3":"map runs on the filtered items, not the remainders."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('4b82b60c-074d-5158-8647-dfd13c078078', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The block-bodied arrow updates acc but returns undefined, so from the second item onward acc is undefined and the final result is undefined. Add return acc.$c$, $c${"1":"An object is the right accumulator for a tally.","2":"?? works with any values.","3":"reduce works on arrays of any element type."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('6afd48ed-ff97-5264-a7fb-41b4bd9ff15a', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The default sort converts to strings: '1' < '10' < '100' < '9'. The numeric comparator sorts by value.$c$, $c${"1":"The default sort is lexicographic, not numeric.","2":"The default sort is ascending in string order.","3":"String order places '1' before '10' before '100' before '9'."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('efde73b6-585c-5617-a03b-b6922a556c0d', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$find returns the first item over 5 (8). some is true because 15 > 10. every is true because all are above 3. every on an empty array is true (vacuous truth).$c$, $c${"1":"find returns one item, not an array, and every([]) is true.","2":"every on an empty array returns true.","3":"15 is greater than 10, so some is true."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('bdd9738a-f7ca-5d0b-8140-8afb9c549a29', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$sort sorts a in place and returns a itself, so c === a. toSorted returns a new sorted array, and spread copies into a new array.$c$, $c${"1":"toSorted never returns the original array.","2":"Spread always creates a new array.","3":"b is a new array; c is the original."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('3793c65b-f9e3-5917-a408-957666237d53', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$toUpperCase's result was discarded and index assignment on a string silently does nothing. slice(-3) takes the last three characters and at(0) the first.$c$, $c${"1":"The uppercase result was never stored.","2":"Index assignment on a string has no effect.","3":"A negative slice index counts from the end."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('bd2a00dd-c359-5948-ba00-032b1d396973', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Splitting 'a,b,,c' on commas yields ['a', 'b', '', 'c'] (length 4). filter(Boolean) removes the empty string and join adds hyphens.$c$, $c${"1":"The empty segment between the two commas counts as an element.","2":"filter(Boolean) removed the empty string before joining.","3":"join('-') inserts hyphens between elements."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('58e290a5-dfe0-5373-9fd9-afbeae2baedf', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$replace with a string pattern replaces only the first occurrence. replaceAll replaces every occurrence. The template literal evaluates 1 + 1 to 2.$c$, $c${"1":"replace with a string replaces only the first match.","2":"Template literals evaluate the expression inside ${}.","3":"replace does change the first hyphen."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('48e2c297-323c-578a-aea9-160a155d0e8d', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$NaN === NaN is always false. Number.isNaN(v) is true because v is the NaN value. The global isNaN('12') converts to 12, not NaN, so false. Number.isNaN('abc') is false because the string is not the NaN value.$c$, $c${"1":"NaN never equals anything, and Number.isNaN does not convert strings.","2":"isNaN('12') coerces to the number 12, which is not NaN.","3":"Number.isNaN(NaN) is true."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('7be56547-9f0c-559a-ad7c-07d5e2949f00', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004, so it is not === 0.3 and it is slightly greater than 0.3. toFixed(2) rounds the display to '0.30'.$c$, $c${"1":"The sum is not exactly 0.3.","2":"toFixed(2) rounds the tiny error away, giving '0.30'.","3":"The sum is slightly more than 0.3."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('63ff0264-845d-57f2-b039-39020a520417', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$parseInt reads '12' and stops at '.'. Number needs the whole string to be numeric, so NaN. parseFloat reads 12.9. Math.round(12.5) is 13 and Math.round(-12.5) is -12 because .5 rounds toward +Infinity.$c$, $c${"1":"parseInt returns an integer, and -12.5 rounds up to -12.","2":"Number('12.9px') is NaN, and Math.round(12.5) is 13.","3":"parseInt accepts the leading digits."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('9ca9eda6-de6b-51b3-890d-3516a6bfd780', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Object properties whose values are undefined or functions are skipped. NaN becomes null. Inside arrays, undefined becomes null so positions are preserved.$c$, $c${"1":"Undefined and function properties are omitted from objects, not converted to null.","2":"NaN becomes null, and array positions are kept as null.","3":"None of these values cause an error; only circular references or BigInt do."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b9c259cc-df0d-5db3-9f52-37b616c024c3', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Date has a toJSON method that produces an ISO string. JSON has no date type, so parse returns the string; a reviver is needed to rebuild a Date.$c$, $c${"1":"JSON cannot represent Date objects.","2":"The date becomes an ISO string, not a timestamp number.","3":"The parsed value is a primitive string."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('226978ca-24dc-5cc7-993e-5b81858a4e1c', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$null is converted to the string 'null', which is valid JSON and parses to null. The line can still throw if the stored text is malformed, so a try/catch is still advisable.$c$, $c${"1":"'null' is valid JSON.","2":"JSON.parse never returns undefined for valid input.","3":"Corrupt stored text would make JSON.parse throw."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('95b7c263-c3ab-549b-99be-f9a313069b0f', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The return value 'try' is computed, then finally runs and logs 'cleanup', then the function returns and the caller logs 'try'.$c$, $c${"1":"finally executes before control leaves the function.","2":"finally always runs, even after a return.","3":"The returned value is still 'try' and gets logged."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('cf164272-cb5d-56d4-a7de-bb0aea124b12', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The callback runs later from the task queue, outside the try block, so the catch cannot see it. 'after' prints first and the error is reported as uncaught when the timer fires.$c$, $c${"1":"The throw happens after the try block has finished.","2":"The catch block never runs.","3":"'after' is printed synchronously."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('14ef8d98-0024-50e4-8fcb-a5bf9bfd741b', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Error instances capture the stack, have a name and message, and can be distinguished with instanceof. Strings lack all of that and make debugging and selective handling harder.$c$, $c${"1":"Any value can be thrown in any mode.","2":"catch receives whatever was thrown, including strings.","3":"An uncaught throw of any type is reported the same way."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('39bfdde1-989b-53cb-b069-45c437bf7765', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Stack traces list the most recent call first. The error occurred in readTotal at line 8; the frames below show how execution got there.$c$, $c${"1":"The bottom frame is the outermost caller, not the failure point.","2":"submit called readTotal; the failure is inside readTotal.","3":"All frames are in your own file here."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('08c093f6-d6fd-5e85-aa3c-8498cabdaa31', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The console keeps a reference to the object. When you expand it after the mutation, it reads the current properties. Log a copy (structuredClone or JSON.stringify) to freeze the snapshot.$c$, $c${"1":"console.log runs synchronously; the display is what is lazy.","2":"Primitives are shown as-is; only object expansion is live.","3":"Assignments are not hoisted."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('c720d575-2047-5bba-8a73-314548ac7a28', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$DevTools supports conditional breakpoints (and a debugger statement inside an if), which stop only for the matching iteration and let you inspect scope directly.$c$, $c${"1":"Logging works but is noisy and does not let you inspect scope.","2":"Resuming repeatedly is slow for large loops.","3":"try/catch does not pause execution."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('0a27c3aa-74f2-51b5-8986-665ace9f3e72', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$User-provided text assigned to innerHTML can inject elements and event handlers. textContent renders the characters literally and is safe for untrusted input.$c$, $c${"1":"Performance is not the issue; injection is.","2":"innerHTML accepts strings; that is exactly the danger.","3":"innerHTML must not be used for untrusted text."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('3d30a4c4-dab3-50a2-a524-b567c4e175de', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$querySelector returns the first matching element, whose tagName is uppercase 'LI' for HTML documents. querySelectorAll returns a NodeList of length 2. No match returns null, not undefined.$c$, $c${"1":"querySelector returns a single element and null (not undefined) for no match.","2":"tagName is uppercase in HTML documents.","3":"querySelectorAll returns every match."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('65353b8c-5291-58ae-8620-366e884bcc45', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Toggling 'active' on and then off leaves only 'btn'. classList.add does not duplicate an existing class. dataset.orderId reads data-order-id as the string '77'.$c$, $c${"1":"The second toggle removed 'active'.","2":"classList never stores duplicates.","3":"dataset values are always strings."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('247c1512-4360-5037-92e5-04928b2fcf48', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$first is already in the list, so append moves it to the end rather than copying. The count stays 2 and the order becomes b, a.$c$, $c${"1":"append does not clone; use cloneNode for a copy.","2":"The node moved to the end, changing the order.","3":"No node was duplicated."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b2562466-b0bf-50b7-99f9-5bcf25efb480', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Appending many nodes individually can trigger repeated reflows. Collecting them in a fragment and appending once keeps the DOM update to a single insertion.$c$, $c${"1":"Appending one at a time works; it is just less efficient.","2":"Fragments have nothing to do with events.","3":"textContent works on any element regardless of where it is."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('86722b13-1184-5591-9320-009f846b8840', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$replaceChildren() replaces all children with nothing. remove() would remove the container itself; innerHTML is a string with no clear method; children is an HTMLCollection without forEach.$c$, $c${"1":"remove removes the container, not its children.","2":"innerHTML is a string; strings have no clear method.","3":"HTMLCollection has no forEach; you would need Array.from first."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b54e80d8-b469-591a-9da8-3d75325bde55', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The click originates on the span, so target is label. The event bubbles to the div whose listener is running, so currentTarget is box.$c$, $c${"1":"target is the innermost element clicked.","2":"currentTarget is the element with the listener, the div.","3":"The roles are reversed."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('74da49fe-f2b3-5a6c-9226-c199d23d20ab', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Each arrow expression creates a new function. Store the handler in a variable and pass the same reference to both calls.$c$, $c${"1":"The options argument is optional as long as capture matches.","2":"Any listener can be removed with a matching reference.","3":"Arrow functions can be removed if the same reference is used."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('15837403-52f8-57bd-a0e6-fccc4ac35a13', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Because clicks bubble up from any li to the list, one listener covers every row, including rows added later, and avoids attaching a listener per item.$c$, $c${"1":"The pattern does not stop propagation.","2":"Speed of closest is not the point; fewer listeners and future rows are.","3":"It uses a data attribute to identify the row."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('0c2e9fdd-e473-57c0-aa23-4db5b0e7119b', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Even for type=number, .value is the string '5', so '5' + 1 concatenates to '51'. valueAsNumber returns 5 and 5 + 1 is 6.$c$, $c${"1":"input.value is a string regardless of the input type.","2":"String + number concatenates.","3":"typeof value is 'string'."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('a5be7981-4e51-5738-822e-59be5aeb7a8d', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Named fields appear in FormData. A checked checkbox contributes its value attribute ('yes'); an unchecked one is omitted entirely. Without a value attribute it would be 'on'.$c$, $c${"1":"FormData carries the string value, not a boolean.","2":"Named fields are included.","3":"'on' is only the default when no value attribute is set."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b88e5b95-2c4d-54d6-93d2-264c740c1112', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The input event fires immediately whenever the value changes, including typing, pasting, and deleting. change waits until the field loses focus or the value is committed.$c$, $c${"1":"change fires on blur/commit, not per keystroke.","2":"submit fires on the form, not on typing.","3":"keyup fires on every key, not just Enter, and misses paste."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('297d98dc-8c4d-51ec-91ca-941f4ea7932a', $c${"expected":"1\n5\n3\n4\n2"}$c$::jsonb, $c$Synchronous logs 1 and 5 run first. Both the promise reaction and queueMicrotask are microtasks and run in the order queued (3, then 4) before the timer task logs 2.$c$, $c${}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('44af1504-cdbb-5392-85ea-e9066f9c725f', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The busy loop blocks the thread for 500 ms. The timer's task is queued at 100 ms but cannot run until the synchronous code finishes, so 'loop over' prints first and 'done' follows.$c$, $c${"1":"Nothing can interrupt synchronous code.","2":"The timer has a 100 ms minimum delay.","3":"The callback runs once the stack is clear."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('1d2cccf6-3e9b-537d-8a4e-72f98d155132', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$clearInterval() without an argument does nothing. Store the return value of setInterval in a variable and pass it to clearInterval.$c$, $c${"1":"Clearing from inside the callback is common and fine with the id.","2":"let is correct here.","3":"Intervals repeat until cleared."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('397ae61d-3e3f-5894-bf33-e9222ef7ced7', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$A 404 is a valid HTTP response, so the promise resolves. ok is false because the status is outside 200–299. The catch runs only for network-level failures.$c$, $c${"1":"HTTP error statuses do not reject the fetch promise.","2":"No exception is thrown.","3":"ok is false for 404."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('59878d7b-8fd2-517d-a2a9-bbd34acdeb60', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$The second fetch does not start until the first await resolves. Starting both and awaiting Promise.all([fetch('/a'), fetch('/b')]) would take about 1 second.$c$, $c${"1":"The second fetch is not called until the first resolves.","2":"Non-blocking does not mean instant; the function still waits for both.","3":"The order is fixed by the awaits."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('63605f64-b052-5500-904a-e3b236941506', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Without await, user is a Promise object, which has no name property, so typeof user.name is 'undefined'. Write const user = await res.json().$c$, $c${"1":"The data has not been read yet; user is a promise.","2":"user.name is undefined on a promise.","3":"json() takes no arguments."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('3766a113-daed-5681-b67b-d326d1adc3be', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$A default export is imported without braces, or with braces as { default as main }. The other three forms are all valid ways to import from this module.$c$, $c${"1":"A default import can use any local name.","2":"Combining a default and named imports is allowed.","3":"Namespace imports include the default under util.default."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('702bd1c5-394c-5351-828d-3c140efaf2ce', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$count in app.js reflects the current value of the exported variable, which bump changed twice. Reading it is fine; assigning to an imported binding is what throws.$c$, $c${"1":"Imports are not copies made at import time.","2":"count is defined and exported.","3":"Reading an import is allowed; only assignment throws."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8d91bb90-25d4-5f3b-a30b-a6cd27ae16ae', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Module scripts wait for the document to be parsed (like defer), always run in strict mode, and keep top-level declarations private. They use import, not require.$c$, $c${"1":"Module scripts do not block parsing.","2":"Module scope is private.","3":"require is CommonJS, not available in browser modules."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('d7f5f33a-ca5e-54d9-8ec3-8cc404cab0ec', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$setItem converts the value to a string, and an object's default string form is '[object Object]'. Use JSON.stringify when saving and JSON.parse when reading.$c$, $c${"1":"No JSON conversion happens automatically.","2":"getItem always returns a string or null.","3":"The key was set, just with an unhelpful value."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('b23895a0-9f3f-5f90-b6b7-8c3bf0d3ff37', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$localStorage lasts until cleared and is shared by all tabs of the origin; sessionStorage is per tab and ends with it. Neither is sent with requests (that is cookies), and both store strings only.$c$, $c${"1":"sessionStorage is scoped to a single tab.","2":"Cookies travel with requests; web storage does not.","3":"Values must be strings."}$c$::jsonb, 1),
('8c231308-f611-518c-840f-7fad65fd0b1b', $c${"correct":[0]}$c$::jsonb, $c$Keeping all DOM updates inside render guarantees the screen always matches state and storage. Handlers that touch the DOM directly cause drift between what is shown and what is saved.$c$, $c${"1":"Direct DOM edits in handlers bypass the single source of truth.","2":"Saving should not know about the DOM.","3":"load runs once; toggles happen later."}$c$::jsonb, 1)
